1
00:01:28,090 --> 00:01:30,680
<i>Obviously,
I miscalculated a few things.</i>

2
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:41,690
Eddie! I know you're in there!

3
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,910
<i>Why is it that the moment your life
exceeds your wildest dreams...</i>

4
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:52,530
<i>The access code you have entered...</i>

5
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:54,910
<i>...the knife appears at your back?</i>

6
00:01:54,990 --> 00:01:58,290
<i>The access code
you have entered is invalid...</i>

7
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:01,790
<i>Well, I'll tell you one thing...</i>

8
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:26,400
<i>...I will never let them touch me.</i>

9
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:42,120
<i>My neighbor, who must have
opened his door to complain.</i>

10
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:45,710
You're gonna be next, Eddie!
We're coming in!

11
00:02:47,630 --> 00:02:51,550
<i>For a guy with a four digit IQ,
I must have missed something.</i>

12
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:53,510
<i>And I hadn't missed much.</i>

13
00:02:54,930 --> 00:02:57,470
<i>I'd come this close to having
an impact on the world.</i>

14
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:01,390
<i>And now, the only thing
I'd have an impact on...</i>

15
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,190
<i>...was the sidewalk.</i>

16
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:27,850
<i>You see that guy?</i>

17
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:29,940
<i>That was me, not so long ago.</i>

18
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:33,940
<i>What kind of guy without a drug or
alcohol problem looks this way?</i>

19
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:36,740
<i>Only a writer. Strangely enough,</i>

20
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:39,490
<i>nobody believed that
I actually had a book contract.</i>

21
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:41,280
Well, it's masquerading
as a sci-fi novel,

22
00:04:41,370 --> 00:04:43,040
but it's really, uh,
my own personal manifesto

23
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:45,040
about the plight of the
individual in the 21st century.

24
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:47,370
I sort of created a utopian society

25
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:49,670
where we all sort of...
Uh... Uh...

26
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:52,460
It's really... it's really...

27
00:04:56,260 --> 00:04:57,220
Uh...

28
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:02,720
<i>Today was the day
I was gonna kick its ass.</i>

29
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:05,810
<i>Here we go.
Here we go. Here we go...</i>

30
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:08,980
<i>It was gonna be great.</i>

31
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,270
<i>Just gotta put in the time.
Stay in the room.</i>

32
00:05:13,770 --> 00:05:15,070
<i>That's the key concept.</i>

33
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:18,610
<i>Don't leave the room.</i>

34
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:28,660
<i>A few weeks passed this way.</i>

35
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:30,080
<i>Maybe a few months.</i>

36
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:37,210
<i>At least I still had Lindy.</i>

37
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:42,050
Just like that?

38
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:47,520
Come on, Eddie.
This is hardly a surprise.

39
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:50,020
I'm surprised.

40
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:53,270
Let's just not do this, OK?
Let me...

41
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,070
I'm gonna hand in
90 pages to Marla on Friday.

42
00:05:55,150 --> 00:05:56,610
If you just wait to see
what she says...

43
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:57,900
Eddie.

44
00:05:57,990 --> 00:06:00,200
- What?
- I know how it's going.

45
00:06:00,990 --> 00:06:02,030
I'm your...

46
00:06:02,870 --> 00:06:05,080
I was your girlfriend.

47
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,580
That word doesn't even begin
to describe what you are to me.

48
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:12,040
Partner? Squeeze?

49
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:15,000
Paramour. Inamorata.

50
00:06:15,090 --> 00:06:18,300
Cleaning lady. Bank.

51
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,220
Be that a...

52
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:25,600
What's mine is yours, too,

53
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:28,100
and you know I want to share
the rest of my life with you.

54
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:30,350
- I was thinking that we should...
- Don't propose.

55
00:06:31,270 --> 00:06:32,270
Why not?

56
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:36,400
Because it worked out
so well for you last time.

57
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:39,610
<i>It's true, I did marry once.
Right out of college, to Melissa.</i>

58
00:06:39,690 --> 00:06:41,280
I do.

59
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:42,360
<i>Briefly.</i>

60
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,240
This isn't working.

61
00:06:49,250 --> 00:06:51,580
Well, even though everything you
say is true, I still love you.

62
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,460
I gotta get back to work.

63
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:03,680
You didn't even
tell me what happened.

64
00:07:04,430 --> 00:07:06,100
- I got it.
- You did?

65
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:08,470
Mmm-hm. I'm an editor.

66
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,980
I'll have my own assistant.
Can you believe that?

67
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:13,690
Yeah, of course I believe it.
You deserve it.

68
00:07:16,110 --> 00:07:17,230
Thanks.

69
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:21,740
- OK. - OK.

70
00:07:29,290 --> 00:07:31,410
<i>She was right. Why stick it out?</i>

71
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:34,370
<i>I had clearly missed the on-ramp.</i>

72
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:36,830
<i>We both knew what was beckoning.</i>

73
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:40,170
<i>The lower bunk in my
childhood bedroom in Jersey.</i>

74
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:42,840
<i>My father happy to welcome me
into the challenging field</i>

75
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:45,010
<i>of dental supply inventory.</i>

76
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:46,840
Eddie Morra.

77
00:07:48,930 --> 00:07:51,180
<i>Of all the useless
relationships better forgotten</i>

78
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:52,810
<i>and put away in mothballs,</i>

79
00:07:52,890 --> 00:07:55,850
<i>is there any more useless than...
the ex-brother-in-law?</i>

80
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:58,190
Holy shit, man.

81
00:07:58,270 --> 00:08:00,190
What has it been? Like nine years?

82
00:08:00,270 --> 00:08:01,530
- God! - Hey.

83
00:08:02,610 --> 00:08:05,200
You all right? You look like
you're living on the streets.

84
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:06,280
What's going on?

85
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:09,490
Not much, I... Well...
What am I... What's up?

86
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:11,660
Uh, I'm writing. Writing a lot.

87
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:13,370
Oh, you're still trying to write?

88
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:15,660
Matter of fact,
I have a book contract.

89
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:17,540
- Really? - Yeah.

90
00:08:17,630 --> 00:08:19,130
- That's great.
- How about you?

91
00:08:19,210 --> 00:08:20,630
Are you still dealing, Vern?

92
00:08:20,710 --> 00:08:24,010
- Do I look like I'm still dealing?
- No, you don't. No.

93
00:08:24,510 --> 00:08:26,340
Come on, let's go get a drink.
I want to hear about this book.

94
00:08:27,130 --> 00:08:29,350
I don't know.
It's 2:00 in the afternoon.

95
00:08:29,930 --> 00:08:32,890
When has that ever stopped you?

96
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:37,730
- So. - So...

97
00:08:37,810 --> 00:08:39,440
How's Melissa doing?

98
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:41,190
- Here you go.
- Thanks.

99
00:08:41,270 --> 00:08:45,400
Wouldn't know. I don't see her.
She moved upstate.

100
00:08:45,490 --> 00:08:50,070
She got some kind of Internet home sales
kind of job. Couple kids.

101
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:53,120
Couple kids. Who's the husband?

102
00:08:54,120 --> 00:08:57,710
He walked out on her,
if you really want to know.

103
00:08:57,790 --> 00:08:59,210
But what do you care, man?

104
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,460
You were married, like, five minutes.

105
00:09:02,550 --> 00:09:05,460
Look, I don't wanna talk about her.
I wanna talk about you, OK?

106
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:08,340
Tell me about this book.
How's it going?

107
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:11,600
Well, it's, uh...

108
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:13,060
I'm... I'm...

109
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:15,980
I'm behind.
I'm behind on my book.

110
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:20,310
And, uh, it's pretty well polluting my days
and nights, if you really want to know.

111
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:23,860
- Well, how much have you written of it?
- Not one word.

112
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:27,360
- Creative problems, huh?
- Yeah. Mm-hm.

113
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:33,240
Well, I suppose
I can help you with that.

114
00:09:33,330 --> 00:09:35,040
Just this once.

115
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:37,660
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

116
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:41,250
- You don't even know what it is.
- You're still dealing.

117
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:43,920
Brother,
I am light years from that now.

118
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,130
I've been doing, uh, some consulting
for a pharmaceutical company.

119
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:50,970
What, like some offshore lab
making fake Viagra? Come on, Vern.

120
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:55,890
No. This is an exclusive product
that's coming on stream next year.

121
00:09:55,970 --> 00:09:58,850
They've had clinical trials
and it's FDA approved.

122
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:01,850
All right, just out of curiosity
and that's all...

123
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:05,190
Let's see it.

124
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:09,320
What's in it?

125
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:12,240
They've identified these receptors
in the brain

126
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:15,120
that activate specific circuits.

127
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:18,870
And you how they say that we can
only access 20 percent of our brain?

128
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:24,040
Well, what this does...
it lets you access all of it.

129
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:28,670
Vern, look at me. Do I look good to you?
I'm broke and I'm depressed off my ass.

130
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:31,300
I don't think that my life's
gonna take some sudden upswing

131
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:34,640
into fame and fortune by taking some shiny,
brand-new designer drug.

132
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:37,810
Gant. When?

133
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:41,940
Tell him we can't do that.
No, you tell him.

134
00:10:43,770 --> 00:10:44,980
No, now.

135
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:50,190
Well, I'm afraid I'm gonna
have to leave you here, Eddie.

136
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:52,950
But I really... I really do
want to do this again.

137
00:10:53,030 --> 00:10:56,330
So, call me.
And, uh, that's on the house.

138
00:10:57,200 --> 00:10:58,620
I don't want it.

139
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:01,910
Don't be ungrateful.
You know how much that costs?

140
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,630
Eight hundred bucks. A pop.

141
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:06,880
You're welcome.

142
00:11:18,430 --> 00:11:20,470
<i>All the way home,
I thought about Melissa.</i>

143
00:11:21,230 --> 00:11:23,890
<i>How could she be a failure, too?
She'd been so smart.</i>

144
00:11:24,770 --> 00:11:27,860
<i>Smarter than anyone around her.</i>

145
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:30,320
<i>Clearly, there was a direct link
between this Eddie,</i>

146
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:33,490
<i>slightly drunk at 3:00 in the afternoon,
and an earlier Eddie...</i>

147
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:37,620
<i>...vomiting on his boss's desk
or stealing his dying aunt's Percocet.</i>

148
00:11:39,330 --> 00:11:42,370
<i>So who could blame Lindy
for dumping me?</i>

149
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:54,340
<i>In the end, how much worse could it get?</i>

150
00:12:02,730 --> 00:12:04,730
<i>I didn't want to see anybody.</i>

151
00:12:04,810 --> 00:12:07,650
<i>Especially not my landlord's
nasty young wife.</i>

152
00:12:07,730 --> 00:12:10,070
- Valerie, Tuesday.
- Look. Enough, OK?

153
00:12:10,150 --> 00:12:12,440
Steve handles the rent,
so you can feed your crap to him.

154
00:12:12,530 --> 00:12:14,450
<i>I suddenly had extra reason
to get away from her.</i>

155
00:12:14,530 --> 00:12:17,320
<i>I had thoughtlessly ingested
a substance.</i>

156
00:12:17,410 --> 00:12:18,780
Like the rent's not low enough!

157
00:12:18,870 --> 00:12:21,540
<i>I had gotten remarkably
little information from Vernon</i>

158
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:23,330
<i>about what this drug would do.</i>

159
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:27,000
You could even be a bike messenger
and come up with that. Really.

160
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:29,380
<i>- What if it was a hallucinogen?
- It's pathetic.</i>

161
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:31,840
<i>Oh, my God.
Listening to her rag...</i>

162
00:12:31,920 --> 00:12:33,590
<i>If I were tripping,
I'd jump out a fucking window.</i>

163
00:12:33,670 --> 00:12:35,840
Hello? Are you listening to me?

164
00:12:35,930 --> 00:12:38,930
Do you get it?
You are gonna be out on the streets!

165
00:12:39,010 --> 00:12:40,760
<i>And then, I felt it.</i>

166
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:14,050
<i>I was blind, but now I see.</i>

167
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:31,610
...running away!
You can't even look at me, you know why?

168
00:13:31,690 --> 00:13:33,900
Because you're not even a man.
You're not even a man!

169
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:37,360
You don't have rent!
You don't have a job!

170
00:13:48,710 --> 00:13:50,830
- What's wrong?
- What?

171
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:53,380
You don't like me and I don't blame you.
You see a self-defeating,

172
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:56,170
energy-sucking piece of shit
who's sponging off your husband.

173
00:13:56,260 --> 00:13:57,590
You're wishing I'd blow my brains out,

174
00:13:57,670 --> 00:14:00,590
but my existence shouldn't make you
this upset. What is it?

175
00:14:01,590 --> 00:14:04,310
- That's none of your business.
- Something wrong at law school?

176
00:14:04,390 --> 00:14:05,760
How do you know I'm in law school?

177
00:14:05,850 --> 00:14:07,470
People who aren't, usually
don't carry around dry,

178
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:10,100
academically-constipated books
about a dead Supreme Court justice.

179
00:14:10,810 --> 00:14:13,150
You're a creep, aren't you?
You've been... You've been following me.

180
00:14:13,230 --> 00:14:16,280
- No, I just noticed the book.
- You just saw the corner of it.

181
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:17,740
How did you know that?

182
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:20,910
<i>I'd seen it before.
Twelve years ago in college.</i>

183
00:14:20,990 --> 00:14:23,240
<i>Sitting on the couch of a TA
I was trying to make,</i>

184
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:25,080
<i>waiting for her to come
back out of the bathroom,</i>

185
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:27,120
<i>hoping she'd have a condom.</i>

186
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:29,750
<i>Somehow my unconscious
had served that up.</i>

187
00:14:29,830 --> 00:14:31,370
<i>A memory I had never even recorded.</i>

188
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:35,170
<i>Or was it there the whole time
and all I needed was the access?</i>

189
00:14:35,250 --> 00:14:37,380
If you're writing a paper,
that's not the book I'd use.

190
00:14:37,460 --> 00:14:39,260
Well, who asked you?

191
00:14:39,340 --> 00:14:41,260
Hastings has his oral history.
I'd start there.

192
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:43,430
Interesting point.
Grammatically, this guy was an idiot,

193
00:14:43,510 --> 00:14:44,850
which sort of gives credence
to the theory

194
00:14:44,930 --> 00:14:45,930
that one of the clerks he had fired

195
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:48,140
actually wrote most of
this guy's major opinions.

196
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,560
You could Google the clerk's sons,
they'd love to talk to you.

197
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:53,310
Exonerate their dad. That'd give you
something that no one else has...

198
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:56,150
<i>Information from the odd
museum show, a half-read article,</i>

199
00:14:56,230 --> 00:14:59,280
<i>some PBS documentary... it was all
bubbling up in my frontal lobes,</i>

200
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:02,780
<i>mixing itself together into a sparkling
cocktail of useful information.</i>

201
00:15:04,870 --> 00:15:07,700
<i>- She didn't have a chance.</i>
- So, what are your suggestions?

202
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:14,790
<i>We'd really worked
on her paper, too.</i>

203
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:19,210
<i>In 45 minutes, it was a polished gem.
She was pleased.</i>

204
00:15:25,430 --> 00:15:29,220
<i>Home. But it couldn't be my home,
could it?</i>

205
00:15:30,060 --> 00:15:31,520
<i>Who would live like this?</i>

206
00:15:33,140 --> 00:15:35,560
<i>Torch it.</i>

207
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:38,400
<i>But saner heads prevailed.</i>

208
00:16:13,850 --> 00:16:16,190
<i>What was this drug?
I couldn't stay messy on it,</i>

209
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:21,110
<i>I hadn't had a cigarette in six hours,
hadn't eaten, so... abstemious and tidy?</i>

210
00:16:21,190 --> 00:16:24,650
<i>What was this? A drug for people who
wanted to be more anal retentive?</i>

211
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:27,530
<i>I wasn't high, I wasn't wired.
Just clear.</i>

212
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:30,120
<i>I knew what I needed to do
and how to do it.</i>

213
00:17:07,910 --> 00:17:13,330
<i>The next morning, I sent
a little probe down into my brain.</i>

214
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:17,000
<i>No surge of brilliance
came up to greet me.</i>

215
00:17:20,380 --> 00:17:23,630
<i>In short... I was back.</i>

216
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:30,220
<i>But, something remained.</i>

217
00:17:34,350 --> 00:17:35,350
You're kidding.

218
00:17:36,310 --> 00:17:39,440
- No.
- Words have appeared on paper.

219
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:42,480
- Yes.
- Written by you.

220
00:17:42,570 --> 00:17:43,940
All you have to do is read three pages.

221
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:47,110
Just read three pages
in the next hour and...

222
00:17:47,190 --> 00:17:49,740
...if you don't want to keep reading,
I'll give you back the advance.

223
00:17:53,370 --> 00:17:54,870
OK.

224
00:17:56,250 --> 00:17:58,460
- OK, Eddie. - OK.

225
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:27,400
<i>Eddie,
give me a call when you get in.</i>

226
00:18:29,490 --> 00:18:34,120
<i>Eddie, I'm 40 pages further in.
Call me.</i>

227
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:37,330
<i>It's a little grandiose,
but I'm still reading.</i>

228
00:18:37,410 --> 00:18:41,170
<i>OK, how did you do this?</i>

229
00:18:41,250 --> 00:18:46,420
<i>I'd really like... Just call me
the minute you get in. Call. OK?</i>

230
00:19:02,440 --> 00:19:05,690
- Who is it?
- Hey, Vernon, it's Eddie.

231
00:19:05,770 --> 00:19:10,280
- Eddie who?
- Uh, Morra.

232
00:19:12,530 --> 00:19:14,700
It's not a good time, Eddie.

233
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:17,950
Uh, hey, Vern, I really
just need to talk to you.

234
00:19:19,410 --> 00:19:21,120
Hey... Oh...

235
00:19:24,170 --> 00:19:27,800
- Vern, what happened?
- Don't worry about it. I'm fine.

236
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:31,760
So, Eddie.
You are interested after all.

237
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:35,430
- That stuff's amazing.
- Works better if you're already smart.

238
00:19:36,430 --> 00:19:38,140
Vern, who did this to you?

239
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:40,140
Trust me, you don't want to know.

240
00:19:41,810 --> 00:19:44,560
Uh...

241
00:19:44,650 --> 00:19:48,690
- So, uh, what's it called?
- Doesn't have a street name yet.

242
00:19:48,770 --> 00:19:52,570
But the boys in the kitchen
are calling it NZT 48.

243
00:19:52,650 --> 00:19:55,860
"The boys in the kitchen"?
I don't...

244
00:19:55,950 --> 00:19:59,330
That doesn't sound
very FDA-approved, Vern.

245
00:19:59,410 --> 00:20:04,080
FDA-approved, that's a laugh. You didn't
really believe that shit, did ya?

246
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:07,080
<i>OK, so what do we have here?</i>

247
00:20:07,170 --> 00:20:10,340
<i>Some unknown, untested,
possibly dangerous drug</i>

248
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:12,760
<i>scammed out of some
unidentified lab somewhere</i>

249
00:20:12,840 --> 00:20:17,220
<i>given to me by a highly unreliable guy
I hadn't seen in years.</i>

250
00:20:17,300 --> 00:20:20,180
- So you want some more?
- Yes. Definitely.

251
00:20:22,180 --> 00:20:26,100
All right, we'll talk about it,
but I, uh, need a favor first.

252
00:20:26,190 --> 00:20:29,400
Obviously, I'm in no condition
to go out right now, so,

253
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:35,110
I was wondering if you could pop down to
the dry cleaners, grab my suit for me.

254
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:39,580
And maybe grab a little...
a little breakfast for us.

255
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:42,830
<i>He knew he had me.</i>

256
00:20:43,580 --> 00:20:44,580
Sure.

257
00:20:45,750 --> 00:20:49,500
<i>From now on,
I'd happily be his errand boy.</i>

258
00:20:49,590 --> 00:20:52,880
<i>Wash his windows,
scrub his toilet...</i>

259
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:55,720
<i>...anything I could do to get my hands
on that little clear pill</i>

260
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:58,890
<i>that would bring back...
Enhanced Eddie.</i>

261
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:11,900
Vern?

262
00:21:22,030 --> 00:21:23,790
Vern?

263
00:21:46,430 --> 00:21:47,850
Vern...

264
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:52,610
oh, Vern.

265
00:22:14,750 --> 00:22:17,050
Hey, hey!

266
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:19,260
Hey!

267
00:22:30,770 --> 00:22:35,230
<i>- 911, what's your emergency?</i>
- Hi, I need to report a...

268
00:22:35,320 --> 00:22:36,780
...a murder.

269
00:22:40,070 --> 00:22:41,700
7B, yes.

270
00:22:44,490 --> 00:22:45,990
OK, I won't.

271
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:53,080
<i>Took them forever. And the longer
I stood there, the clearer I saw.</i>

272
00:22:53,170 --> 00:22:55,290
<i>Vern had known whoever had done this.</i>

273
00:22:56,550 --> 00:22:59,220
<i>And one guess what
they'd been looking for.</i>

274
00:23:03,800 --> 00:23:05,550
<i>But had they found it?</i>

275
00:23:44,970 --> 00:23:48,100
Shit, if you ever cooked,
I'd be dead, too.

276
00:24:22,300 --> 00:24:25,430
Police Department!
Open the door, please!

277
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:30,850
Police Department! Open the door!

278
00:24:34,940 --> 00:24:39,320
Open the door, please!

279
00:24:40,730 --> 00:24:42,860
Police! Open the door!

280
00:24:42,940 --> 00:24:45,860
Let's go! Open the door!

281
00:24:45,950 --> 00:24:47,240
- Let me see your hands.
- Hey.

282
00:24:47,320 --> 00:24:49,280
- Let me see your hands!
- No, no, I'm the one who called!

283
00:24:49,370 --> 00:24:50,780
I called you guys!

284
00:24:53,910 --> 00:24:56,710
So, you guys tight?
Hang out a lot?

285
00:24:56,790 --> 00:25:00,590
No, my ex-wife's brother. I just
actually ran into him on the street

286
00:25:00,670 --> 00:25:03,210
- and he asked me to come up to...
- Buy some drugs?

287
00:25:03,300 --> 00:25:07,180
- No, no. What?
- What did this guy do?

288
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:10,550
He... Uh, I don't know. He was, um...

289
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:13,060
I heard that he was an antiques dealer?

290
00:25:13,140 --> 00:25:16,980
- A dealer?
- Yeah, uh, antiques. Um...

291
00:25:17,060 --> 00:25:20,150
Like, uh, Viennese kinda, um...

292
00:25:21,070 --> 00:25:22,940
...chairs and stuff.

293
00:25:23,030 --> 00:25:25,740
Like, you know,
like curlicue leg things.

294
00:25:25,820 --> 00:25:26,900
Yeah?

295
00:25:27,990 --> 00:25:30,950
That is correct. An Edward Morra.

296
00:25:31,030 --> 00:25:32,370
He's here.

297
00:25:33,330 --> 00:25:35,000
Victim's sister.

298
00:25:35,790 --> 00:25:38,580
<i>I hadn't heard her voice
in ten years.</i>

299
00:25:39,330 --> 00:25:42,040
<i>- Eddie?</i>
- Melissa?

300
00:25:42,750 --> 00:25:45,670
<i>- You were there?</i>
- Um...

301
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:50,840
Just before. I had run into him
on the street and, uh... yeah.

302
00:25:50,930 --> 00:25:56,140
<i>Oh, God. I wish I was more surprised.
He was involved in some stuff.</i>

303
00:25:56,980 --> 00:25:58,440
<i>I'd better not say any more.</i>

304
00:25:58,520 --> 00:26:01,690
No, no, that's probably a good idea.

305
00:26:01,770 --> 00:26:06,320
So, uh... We should probably, uh,
meet or something, right?

306
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:11,120
<i>No, I gotta do the funeral
and God knows what else. I just...</i>

307
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:13,410
<i>We can't meet, OK?</i>

308
00:26:13,490 --> 00:26:15,830
Right. Well, I'll just... I guess I'll
just see you at the funeral then.

309
00:26:15,910 --> 00:26:19,250
<i>No. I don't want that. Please
don't. I'll call you at some point, OK?</i>

310
00:26:19,330 --> 00:26:22,330
<i>- When this is over.</i>
- OK, all right.

311
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:23,630
<i>OK.</i>

312
00:26:28,260 --> 00:26:32,800
You know, something doesn't gel here.

313
00:26:32,890 --> 00:26:36,520
Write down your number.
I may need to contact you later.

314
00:27:09,010 --> 00:27:11,430
<i>Worth the risk?</i>

315
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:18,680
<i>What would you do?</i>

316
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:52,970
<i>Vern's cash, combined with
an unprecedented surge of motivation,</i>

317
00:27:53,050 --> 00:27:55,840
<i>enabled me to finish the book
in four days.</i>

318
00:28:06,440 --> 00:28:07,900
<i>A tablet a day</i>

319
00:28:07,980 --> 00:28:10,400
<i>and what I could do
with my day was limitless.</i>

320
00:28:11,570 --> 00:28:13,860
<i>I learned to play
the piano in three days.</i>

321
00:28:16,200 --> 00:28:17,740
<i>Math became useful.</i>

322
00:28:18,660 --> 00:28:19,740
<i>And fun.</i>

323
00:28:19,830 --> 00:28:21,410
I'm all in.

324
00:28:25,710 --> 00:28:28,670
<i>Even half-listening
to any language, I became fluent.</i>

325
00:28:36,220 --> 00:28:38,720
So that Shakespeare's catchiness
belies mediocrity?

326
00:28:39,560 --> 00:28:40,560
There's no arguing with you.

327
00:28:42,890 --> 00:28:45,190
<i>This was great.</i>

328
00:28:45,270 --> 00:28:47,980
So, Mom, I've been doing a little
research on Aunt Helen's tumor.

329
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:51,650
And it's totally clear that anybody with
familial adenomatous polyposis

330
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:53,940
should be supplementing
platinum-based doublet regiments

331
00:28:54,030 --> 00:28:55,400
with eicosapentaeonic acid.

332
00:28:55,490 --> 00:28:58,030
<i>I suddenly knew everything.
About everything.</i>

333
00:28:58,120 --> 00:29:00,620
Well, sure, you get a short-term spike,
but wouldn't that rapid expansion

334
00:29:00,700 --> 00:29:02,450
devalue the stock completely
in two years?

335
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:05,040
- No, because there are safeguards.
- Against aggressive over-expansion?

336
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,460
There aren't. Because there are
no safeguards in human nature.

337
00:29:07,540 --> 00:29:09,040
We're wired to overreach.

338
00:29:09,130 --> 00:29:11,340
Look at history, all the countries
that ever ruled the world.

339
00:29:11,420 --> 00:29:12,920
Portugal with this big massive navy.

340
00:29:13,010 --> 00:29:14,800
All they got now are salt cod
and cheap condoms.

341
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:15,840
And the Brits?

342
00:29:15,930 --> 00:29:18,010
Now they're just sitting on a dank
little island fussing over their suits.

343
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:19,760
No one's stopping and thinking,
"Hey, we're doing pretty well."

344
00:29:19,850 --> 00:29:22,260
We got France, we got Poland,
we got a big Swiss bank account.

345
00:29:22,350 --> 00:29:24,020
You know what?
Let's not invade Russia in the winter.

346
00:29:24,100 --> 00:29:26,890
"Let's go home, let's pop a beer,
and let's live off the interest."

347
00:29:26,980 --> 00:29:28,400
Yeah.

348
00:29:29,230 --> 00:29:30,860
<i>My brain was just
pouring this stuff out.</i>

349
00:29:30,940 --> 00:29:34,610
<i>Everything I had ever read, heard,
seen was now organized and available.</i>

350
00:29:34,690 --> 00:29:36,200
<i>Here it is. Here you go.</i>

351
00:29:36,820 --> 00:29:39,620
Eddie. Hey. Listen,
I'm sure you have a portfolio,

352
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:42,910
but if you don't, I'd be very
interested in working with you.

353
00:29:42,990 --> 00:29:45,370
OK, Kevin.

354
00:29:45,870 --> 00:29:48,670
<i>I made some new friends
who invited me to the beach.</i>

355
00:29:48,750 --> 00:29:51,090
<i>The beach was not nearby.</i>

356
00:30:03,470 --> 00:30:06,140
<i>All my fear,
all my shyness, gone.</i>

357
00:30:06,230 --> 00:30:08,100
They've done four basic trials...

358
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:11,610
<i>But mere lounging wasn't enough.</i>

359
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:39,800
<i>Only problem?
If I wasn't moving forward,</i>

360
00:30:39,880 --> 00:30:42,140
<i>I felt like I was going to explode.</i>

361
00:30:42,220 --> 00:30:44,350
Come up here because
it's beautiful, right?

362
00:30:44,430 --> 00:30:47,310
- Anybody ever jump?
- Are you crazy?

363
00:30:51,770 --> 00:30:53,730
- He's gonna do it.
- He's not gonna do it.

364
00:30:55,770 --> 00:30:56,820
Oh, my God!

365
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:10,500
Oh!

366
00:31:12,210 --> 00:31:13,380
Ah!

367
00:31:13,460 --> 00:31:17,500
<i>And then I began to form an idea.</i>

368
00:31:17,590 --> 00:31:20,010
<i>Suddenly, I knew exactly
what I needed to do.</i>

369
00:31:20,090 --> 00:31:22,680
<i>It wasn't writing.
It wasn't books.</i>

370
00:31:22,760 --> 00:31:24,800
<i>It was much bigger than that.</i>

371
00:31:27,560 --> 00:31:29,430
<i>But it would take money to get there.</i>

372
00:31:32,390 --> 00:31:34,980
<i>In a down market,
no one was making much.</i>

373
00:31:36,150 --> 00:31:38,480
<i>But no one had NZT.</i>

374
00:31:38,570 --> 00:31:41,700
<i>In an optimistic scenario,
gains of that nature merely would reflect</i>

375
00:31:41,780 --> 00:31:44,660
<i>growth in the economy
and would not pose an obstacle...</i>

376
00:31:51,250 --> 00:31:56,500
<i>Armed with Vern's last $800
I made 2,000 in a day.</i>

377
00:31:56,590 --> 00:31:58,920
<i>Next day, 7, 500.</i>

378
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:02,380
<i>It was too slow.
I'd need more capital.</i>

379
00:32:03,130 --> 00:32:05,430
<i>But banks weren't lending
to guys like me.</i>

380
00:32:05,510 --> 00:32:06,470
Hey, Liev.

381
00:32:06,550 --> 00:32:09,060
That's your man
in the corner back there.

382
00:32:13,190 --> 00:32:14,400
Yeah.

383
00:32:22,570 --> 00:32:27,240
So, uh, Liev told you that
I'm looking for a short-term loan?

384
00:32:27,320 --> 00:32:29,240
Yeah, and I told Liev forget it.

385
00:32:29,790 --> 00:32:30,790
Why?

386
00:32:30,870 --> 00:32:34,960
Because I don't see you before
and I don't fucking like you already.

387
00:32:35,040 --> 00:32:38,000
Why do I give you $100,000?

388
00:32:38,290 --> 00:32:39,630
Because...

389
00:32:41,510 --> 00:32:45,220
...I quintupled my money
four days in a row.

390
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:47,220
So you got a fix on the game.

391
00:32:47,300 --> 00:32:49,050
No, not a fix.

392
00:32:49,140 --> 00:32:51,560
I was able to calculate certain patterns
using algorithms.

393
00:32:51,640 --> 00:32:54,060
If you look at column four,
this was the first...

394
00:32:56,480 --> 00:32:59,480
You're good.

395
00:33:02,110 --> 00:33:03,280
OK, it's a fix.

396
00:33:03,360 --> 00:33:06,240
Yeah, you know,
all you people get caught.

397
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:07,620
What are you gonna do then?

398
00:33:08,450 --> 00:33:10,910
Well, it's not as if I have
a black book with your name in it,

399
00:33:10,990 --> 00:33:12,750
if that's what you're talking about.

400
00:33:12,830 --> 00:33:15,080
And besides, I get the strong feeling

401
00:33:15,160 --> 00:33:18,040
that you're not somebody
I want to disappoint.

402
00:33:23,090 --> 00:33:24,670
Don't get up.
I said don't get up.

403
00:33:24,760 --> 00:33:26,090
- Sit down!
- Hey. OK.

404
00:33:28,090 --> 00:33:31,310
- OK, you take this, you mine.
- OK.

405
00:33:31,390 --> 00:33:35,020
You understand?
You don't pay, you know what we do?

406
00:33:35,100 --> 00:33:37,140
I cut you at waist.

407
00:33:37,230 --> 00:33:40,980
Peel your skin up over your head
and tie knot in it.

408
00:33:41,070 --> 00:33:43,480
You don't die from this.
You suffocate.

409
00:33:43,570 --> 00:33:45,900
- OK. - Good luck.

410
00:33:53,870 --> 00:33:56,660
<i>My new friend, Kevin Doyle,
showed me how you could leverage</i>

411
00:33:56,750 --> 00:34:00,670
<i>two and a half times your cash
at the day trading firm.</i>

412
00:34:00,750 --> 00:34:02,590
<i>I'd been upping the dose
for over a week.</i>

413
00:34:04,880 --> 00:34:07,470
<i>It seemed to cut my learning curve.</i>

414
00:34:21,940 --> 00:34:24,730
Why are you buying that?
The CEO was just indicted.

415
00:34:24,820 --> 00:34:27,650
But not for the big fat defense contract
he bribed his way into.

416
00:34:27,740 --> 00:34:30,160
That's still on.
Should be announced next week.

417
00:34:30,240 --> 00:34:33,120
<i>I was jacked in. Booted up.</i>

418
00:34:33,200 --> 00:34:34,990
<i>At the end of the week,
my brokerage account</i>

419
00:34:35,080 --> 00:34:37,120
<i>contained over two million dollars.</i>

420
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:40,040
<i>Word quickly got out.</i>

421
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:48,880
<i>You have 19 messages.</i>

422
00:34:48,970 --> 00:34:51,470
<i>Four job offers,
my bank raising my line of credit,</i>

423
00:34:51,550 --> 00:34:53,220
<i>a reporter from the New York Post.</i>

424
00:34:53,300 --> 00:34:55,220
<i>Really call me back, Mr. Morra.</i>

425
00:34:55,310 --> 00:34:56,720
<i>No one makes money that fast.</i>

426
00:34:56,810 --> 00:34:59,270
<i>This article's gonna get written
with or without you.</i>

427
00:34:59,350 --> 00:35:02,480
<i>And finally,
Kevin Doyle trying to sound casual.</i>

428
00:35:02,560 --> 00:35:06,980
<i>Eddie, I was talking you up to my boss
and, well, the impossible happened, bud.</i>

429
00:35:07,070 --> 00:35:09,900
<i>We got you a meeting with Carl Van Loon.</i>

430
00:35:09,990 --> 00:35:12,450
Carl Van Loon wants to meet you?

431
00:35:12,530 --> 00:35:16,200
- Apparently so.
- But you're not in the finance game.

432
00:35:16,290 --> 00:35:18,910
What can you do for Carl Van Loon?

433
00:35:39,270 --> 00:35:40,850
Since when do you speak Italian?

434
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:44,440
Oh.
Self-improvement month.

435
00:35:44,520 --> 00:35:46,650
I guess somebody
gave me a wake up call.

436
00:35:47,480 --> 00:35:48,900
I hope you didn't do it for me.

437
00:35:48,980 --> 00:35:51,110
No, I just wanted
to apologize to you, Lin.

438
00:35:52,360 --> 00:35:55,910
And let you know that apparently
my capacity for self-sabotage

439
00:35:55,990 --> 00:35:57,990
wasn't boundless after all.

440
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:02,040
The real question is,
why did you ever put up with me?

441
00:36:03,460 --> 00:36:05,130
I was in love with you.

442
00:36:11,880 --> 00:36:13,430
So, when does the book come out?

443
00:36:13,510 --> 00:36:15,390
Next year.
How's your new assistant?

444
00:36:15,470 --> 00:36:17,140
- She's good.
- Good.

445
00:36:17,220 --> 00:36:19,560
- Yeah. - Yeah.

446
00:36:22,060 --> 00:36:24,060
And, uh, are you gonna get a new...

447
00:36:24,140 --> 00:36:27,270
You know...
I'm really happy you're here with me.

448
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:34,410
I'm proud of you. And...

449
00:36:40,370 --> 00:36:42,120
...a little intimidated.

450
00:36:43,620 --> 00:36:45,750
<i>We started up again.</i>

451
00:36:49,040 --> 00:36:50,340
<i>Her place.</i>

452
00:36:54,380 --> 00:36:55,550
<i>My place.</i>

453
00:36:58,140 --> 00:36:59,600
<i>Basically every place.</i>

454
00:37:03,310 --> 00:37:05,480
"In a sizzling
three days of trading,"

455
00:37:05,560 --> 00:37:08,360
"the unknown Mr. Morra
trumped the market,"

456
00:37:08,440 --> 00:37:11,320
"prompting one top, hedge
fund manager to comment."

457
00:37:11,400 --> 00:37:14,360
"He's Houdini.
He's a prophet of our times."'

458
00:37:14,450 --> 00:37:15,780
OK, give me that.
Give me that!

459
00:37:15,860 --> 00:37:18,070
- "He's God"?
- You coming over later?

460
00:37:18,660 --> 00:37:20,990
I can't. I don't have a key.

461
00:37:21,080 --> 00:37:22,330
Oh, that's right.

462
00:37:22,950 --> 00:37:26,460
- Can I have it back?
- Uh... Nope.

463
00:37:26,540 --> 00:37:28,380
- No?
- I mean, does it have to be that key?

464
00:37:28,460 --> 00:37:30,290
Can it be a new key?

465
00:37:31,420 --> 00:37:33,170
To our place?

466
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:38,840
- It would be cost effective.
- And you say I'm not practical.

467
00:37:50,480 --> 00:37:52,150
Can I walk you to work?

468
00:38:09,460 --> 00:38:11,210
Whatcha doing, hon?

469
00:38:12,090 --> 00:38:13,750
Nothing.

470
00:38:14,880 --> 00:38:16,760
You should sleep.

471
00:38:19,590 --> 00:38:21,470
Isn't your Van Loon meeting tomorrow?

472
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:38,950
Eddie?

473
00:38:39,030 --> 00:38:40,860
Are you OK?

474
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:45,240
What are you doing out here?

475
00:38:49,460 --> 00:38:51,880
When's the last time you ate something?

476
00:38:59,430 --> 00:39:03,220
There he is.
Nice article in the Post, buddy.

477
00:39:05,220 --> 00:39:07,890
OK, he is on his way.

478
00:39:08,600 --> 00:39:11,270
Now, be warned. He's mercurial.

479
00:39:11,690 --> 00:39:14,690
One minute, your best friend,
the next, you're a leper.

480
00:39:14,770 --> 00:39:18,900
And he needs direct answers. Anything
tentative and you've lost him forever.

481
00:39:20,990 --> 00:39:22,660
I think we should rehearse
a few scenarios.

482
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:24,490
I'm eating, Kevin.

483
00:39:25,030 --> 00:39:26,700
Hey, are you up for this?

484
00:39:27,950 --> 00:39:29,960
You sure? 'Cause I got
a little bit on the line here.

485
00:39:30,040 --> 00:39:32,120
Have a toast point.

486
00:39:34,750 --> 00:39:36,670
- It's Carl Van Loon.
- That's Carl Van Loon.

487
00:39:36,750 --> 00:39:37,760
He's worth a fortune.

488
00:39:37,840 --> 00:39:41,010
- Hey, John.
- Hey, Carl. Good to see you.

489
00:39:41,090 --> 00:39:42,680
- How are ya?
- Eddie.

490
00:39:49,770 --> 00:39:52,190
So, Eddie Morra.

491
00:39:52,270 --> 00:39:55,310
- What's your secret?
- Medication.

492
00:39:55,400 --> 00:39:57,110
I'm on special medication.

493
00:39:57,650 --> 00:39:59,150
OK.

494
00:40:01,950 --> 00:40:03,700
Well, it's a formula
with a complex method.

495
00:40:03,780 --> 00:40:06,200
But, of course, research was
a component. Massive research.

496
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:09,330
I knew every helix of every scrap of DNA
in every company I bought.

497
00:40:09,410 --> 00:40:12,040
Papers stacked like the Himalayas
in my office, and you know what?

498
00:40:12,120 --> 00:40:14,250
- The stocks went up.
- By a hair.

499
00:40:14,330 --> 00:40:17,500
Sure, the stocks rose,
but who wants to do all that work

500
00:40:17,590 --> 00:40:19,130
for a shitty little uptake?

501
00:40:19,210 --> 00:40:22,380
It's not the visceral kind of spike
you want in short term holdings so...

502
00:40:22,470 --> 00:40:24,130
...I dispensed
with all the hard statistics

503
00:40:24,220 --> 00:40:25,510
and I went right to the rumor mill.

504
00:40:25,590 --> 00:40:28,720
So you bought your stocks based on what?
The tom-toms beating in the street?

505
00:40:28,810 --> 00:40:32,310
Sure. Share prices aren't really
based on how a company works.

506
00:40:32,390 --> 00:40:33,890
It's about how mass psychology works.

507
00:40:33,980 --> 00:40:36,190
So, if I found certain algorithms
in the perception of the stock...

508
00:40:36,270 --> 00:40:38,570
Pattern recognition.
That's your snake oil.

509
00:40:38,650 --> 00:40:40,940
Well, not everybody
understands the patterns.

510
00:40:41,030 --> 00:40:43,740
And that gave me a little bit more of
an uptake, but it's just a parlor trick.

511
00:40:43,820 --> 00:40:45,410
So then, I went on
to door number three.

512
00:40:45,490 --> 00:40:48,830
Are you really implying that you have
some sort of ultimate formula?

513
00:40:48,910 --> 00:40:52,160
Well, from 12,000 to
2.3 million in ten days.

514
00:40:52,250 --> 00:40:55,710
- I do have a formula, Mr. Pierce.
- Delusions of grandeur.

515
00:40:55,790 --> 00:41:00,000
I don't have delusions of grandeur,
I have an actual recipe for grandeur.

516
00:41:02,970 --> 00:41:06,090
I don't know who you are, Eddie, or what
you game is, but I'm sure of one thing.

517
00:41:06,180 --> 00:41:07,550
You don't work in my world.

518
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:11,060
I'm up to my ass with investment guys
and you don't have their half-terrified,

519
00:41:11,140 --> 00:41:13,180
half-cocky line of crap which is...

520
00:41:13,270 --> 00:41:15,980
...not to say that
I like yours any better.

521
00:41:16,060 --> 00:41:19,020
But you obviously pick your stocks
in a way which I haven't seen before.

522
00:41:19,110 --> 00:41:23,030
Take a look at these companies
and tell me what you think.

523
00:41:23,110 --> 00:41:25,490
- Right now?
- Yeah. Take your time.

524
00:41:27,950 --> 00:41:29,370
Sure.

525
00:41:42,210 --> 00:41:44,880
These companies aren't the question,
are they?

526
00:41:46,340 --> 00:41:47,590
Explain.

527
00:41:47,680 --> 00:41:51,220
Unless you're playing
both sides of the fence.

528
00:41:51,310 --> 00:41:53,140
Control the whole power enchilada,
but, no,

529
00:41:53,220 --> 00:41:55,680
these companies wouldn't get you there
in emerging markets.

530
00:41:55,770 --> 00:41:57,810
You would have to... Oh. Whoa.

531
00:41:57,900 --> 00:42:01,570
This must be some big-ass merger
you're contemplating.

532
00:42:03,730 --> 00:42:05,530
And there's really only one company
with enough bling

533
00:42:05,610 --> 00:42:07,610
to justify a merger
with Van Loon and Associates.

534
00:42:08,910 --> 00:42:10,620
Who have you been talking to?

535
00:42:10,700 --> 00:42:14,330
Oh, I'm just connecting the dots.
Is Hank Atwood really gonna go for it?

536
00:42:14,410 --> 00:42:17,960
Well, you're an amazing con artist,
or you're a very smart young man.

537
00:42:18,040 --> 00:42:20,750
I mean, come on.
The two of you together?

538
00:42:20,830 --> 00:42:22,380
The whole world would have
to come begging for energy

539
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:24,510
like Oliver Twist and his bowl of gruel.

540
00:42:24,590 --> 00:42:27,090
Now you're going backwards.
What else you got?

541
00:42:27,170 --> 00:42:31,430
- A prediction. It won't come off.
- Why's that?

542
00:42:31,510 --> 00:42:34,890
- You'd have to back off Libya.
- Really?

543
00:42:36,100 --> 00:42:39,440
Well, you don't want to hear this from
some schmuck who needs a ride home.

544
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:43,190
All right, you get your shot. Meet me
tomorrow at the Saint Regis at ten

545
00:42:43,270 --> 00:42:45,530
and tell me how the gutsy little schmuck
who needs a ride home

546
00:42:45,610 --> 00:42:48,860
would re-structure these deals.
And you'd better be prepared.

547
00:42:48,950 --> 00:42:50,450
I'm at your disposal.

548
00:42:50,530 --> 00:42:52,530
You don't really live here,
do you?

549
00:42:52,620 --> 00:42:54,830
Well... Spartans weren't
big on amenities.

550
00:42:55,490 --> 00:42:58,120
Yeah, and they eventually
got their asses kicked.

551
00:43:05,380 --> 00:43:10,720
<i>I didn't go in. I wanted
to walk, move, digest, ingest.</i>

552
00:43:10,800 --> 00:43:14,100
<i>There are moments in life... moments
when you know you've crossed a bridge.</i>

553
00:43:14,180 --> 00:43:15,510
<i>Your old life is over.</i>

554
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:18,480
<i>Van Loon was my bridge.
I finally had my shot.</i>

555
00:43:18,560 --> 00:43:22,060
<i>Wall Street would provide my nest egg,
but then how far could I go?</i>

556
00:43:22,150 --> 00:43:25,860
<i>CEO? A global force?
Maybe President.</i>

557
00:43:25,940 --> 00:43:28,690
<i>Time somebody shook up the free world
and got things done.</i>

558
00:43:40,750 --> 00:43:42,500
<i>Suddenly...</i>

559
00:43:44,340 --> 00:43:49,170
<i>It was another skip.
How had I gone the last 20 blocks?</i>

560
00:43:51,380 --> 00:43:55,640
<i>I got another ten blocks.
And then...</i>

561
00:43:58,020 --> 00:44:00,140
<i>...I was way uptown.</i>

562
00:44:43,440 --> 00:44:45,770
<i>What bar was this?</i>

563
00:44:58,410 --> 00:45:01,910
...black canvas and then they would
just drop all the paint...

564
00:45:04,040 --> 00:45:06,460
Yeah. Did you ever meet...

565
00:45:07,590 --> 00:45:10,170
...and then I actually went
there and had lunch, I went back.

566
00:45:10,250 --> 00:45:11,300
My friends are rolling...

567
00:45:13,550 --> 00:45:15,470
<i>I heard you like to party.</i>

568
00:45:39,580 --> 00:45:41,830
- Hey, watch it.
- Hey, hey! Take it easy!

569
00:45:41,910 --> 00:45:44,960
<i>A fight? Don't know how to fight.</i>

570
00:45:45,040 --> 00:45:46,500
<i>Or do I?</i>

571
00:45:55,090 --> 00:45:56,090
Ah!

572
00:45:56,180 --> 00:45:59,010
<i>You use the fishhook action
to get inside the mouth.</i>

573
00:45:59,890 --> 00:46:02,310
<i>Like this.</i>

574
00:46:11,070 --> 00:46:14,940
<i>Attack the sensitive areas.
The eyes, the throat, the groin.</i>

575
00:46:19,620 --> 00:46:22,660
<i>If you are hit, do not stop.</i>

576
00:46:22,740 --> 00:46:25,330
<i>Your persistence will
discourage your attacker.</i>

577
00:46:27,040 --> 00:46:28,170
<i>Off-balancing your opponent</i>

578
00:46:28,250 --> 00:46:31,130
<i>and then using his momentum,
take him down.</i>

579
00:47:02,160 --> 00:47:04,490
<i>When it finally stopped,</i>

580
00:47:04,580 --> 00:47:08,120
<i>I couldn't account
for the last 18 hours of my life.</i>

581
00:47:30,230 --> 00:47:32,900
<i>Hey, it's me.
I thought you were coming over.</i>

582
00:47:32,980 --> 00:47:35,730
<i>Is everything OK?
Call me when you get this.</i>

583
00:48:15,360 --> 00:48:20,150
<i>Even off NZT, I decided
to give Van Loon's files a shot.</i>

584
00:48:27,280 --> 00:48:29,580
<i>They were hieroglyphs.</i>

585
00:48:32,960 --> 00:48:35,540
- Hello?
<i>- Kevin, this is Eddie.</i>

586
00:48:35,630 --> 00:48:36,630
- Eddie?
<i>- Listen,</i>

587
00:48:36,710 --> 00:48:39,670
<i>- I can't make the meeting today.</i>
- What are you talking about?

588
00:48:41,010 --> 00:48:42,380
I can't come in today, I'm sick.

589
00:48:42,470 --> 00:48:44,680
Yeah, well you can't
have the flu right now.

590
00:48:44,760 --> 00:48:46,050
He'll never give you this chance again.

591
00:48:46,140 --> 00:48:51,730
I know, listen, I just...
I have all this data to analyze...

592
00:48:51,810 --> 00:48:54,560
<i>What have you been doing
for the last day?</i>

593
00:48:56,020 --> 00:48:58,900
- Kev, I...
- Don't you get it? This is your test.

594
00:48:59,610 --> 00:49:02,570
Yeah, well, I can't
pass the test right now.

595
00:49:02,650 --> 00:49:04,780
And how am I gonna look if you don't?

596
00:49:04,860 --> 00:49:07,580
<i>- I know, listen, I just...</i>
- Sh. Listen to me.

597
00:49:07,660 --> 00:49:10,410
Pull yourself together.
Get down to the meeting.

598
00:49:16,920 --> 00:49:20,460
- OK, OK.
- I'll be there. I'll be there.

599
00:49:41,940 --> 00:49:44,240
- Hey, how are ya?
- Eddie.

600
00:49:44,320 --> 00:49:45,990
Good to see you again.

601
00:49:47,240 --> 00:49:48,570
Sit down.

602
00:49:52,450 --> 00:49:54,040
What do you know about Hank Atwood?

603
00:49:54,710 --> 00:49:55,960
Atwood, um...

604
00:49:57,330 --> 00:49:59,920
He's an iconoclast, um...

605
00:50:03,010 --> 00:50:04,920
Owns about half of Colorado.

606
00:50:06,430 --> 00:50:08,010
So this is prepared, Eddie?

607
00:50:08,090 --> 00:50:10,100
Uh...

608
00:50:10,180 --> 00:50:13,350
Is this Atwood 101?
Doesn't everybody know about Atwood?

609
00:50:13,430 --> 00:50:15,100
Where was he two years ago?

610
00:50:17,560 --> 00:50:20,440
- Hm? - Nowhere.

611
00:50:22,190 --> 00:50:25,190
Two years ago he wasn't
even on <i>Forbes'</i> radar.

612
00:50:26,570 --> 00:50:29,070
That was a great leap forward, right?

613
00:50:29,950 --> 00:50:33,700
I'm baffled by this guy.
He comes out of nowhere so strong.

614
00:50:33,790 --> 00:50:36,710
Has me on the run before I can
even get to anybody in his camp.

615
00:50:36,790 --> 00:50:38,460
Beats me out of two properties,

616
00:50:38,540 --> 00:50:41,130
investing in countries with no oil.
Places I'd never go near...

617
00:50:48,510 --> 00:50:50,840
<i>We're here in front
of the hotel where a woman,</i>

618
00:50:50,930 --> 00:50:54,680
<i>identified as Maria Winberg,
was found dead in her room last night,</i>

619
00:50:54,770 --> 00:50:59,480
<i>a victim of foul play. Ms. Winberg
was a well-known socialite and model...</i>

620
00:51:00,940 --> 00:51:05,190
<i>...but was also known for her dedicated
charity work around the city.</i>

621
00:51:05,280 --> 00:51:08,450
<i>Police spent hours questioning
hotel staff and patrons</i>

622
00:51:08,530 --> 00:51:11,740
<i>and an eyewitness reported
seeing a man leaving the scene...</i>

623
00:51:11,820 --> 00:51:15,540
...help me convince him that I can
raise his game. Got any ideas?

624
00:51:15,620 --> 00:51:17,370
What do you think?

625
00:51:18,750 --> 00:51:20,580
- Hm? - Um...

626
00:51:20,670 --> 00:51:23,130
<i>...hotel has adequate
security cameras...</i>

627
00:51:27,420 --> 00:51:30,260
You're not one of those
kind of guys, are you Eddie?

628
00:51:30,340 --> 00:51:32,430
We lose you if there's
a screen in the room?

629
00:51:34,680 --> 00:51:37,390
These are tectonic plates
that are shifting right under us.

630
00:51:37,470 --> 00:51:39,100
Pardon me, one second.

631
00:52:01,250 --> 00:52:04,840
<i>Was it possible?
Could I have killed someone?</i>

632
00:52:06,880 --> 00:52:09,010
<i>Was it even me?</i>

633
00:52:13,680 --> 00:52:16,310
<i>Eddie? This is Melissa.</i>

634
00:52:16,390 --> 00:52:18,560
<i>Listen, Vernon hasn't been
giving you anything, has he?</i>

635
00:52:18,640 --> 00:52:20,270
<i>Because that stuff is dangerous.</i>

636
00:52:20,350 --> 00:52:21,810
Melissa?

637
00:52:21,890 --> 00:52:24,230
Melissa, hey, do you think
that we can meet somewhere?

638
00:52:24,310 --> 00:52:26,980
- I really need to talk to you.
<i>- We're talking now.</i>

639
00:52:27,070 --> 00:52:30,400
No, no, let's meet at, uh...
Billy's on Grant at noon?

640
00:52:30,490 --> 00:52:32,820
<i>- You can't see me, Eddie.</i>
- No, no...

641
00:52:32,910 --> 00:52:35,990
<i>- Eddie, please.</i>
- Please, please come, please come, I...

642
00:52:36,080 --> 00:52:39,160
It's important, I really need to know
what you're talking about.

643
00:52:41,750 --> 00:52:45,000
<i>I realized that there were
other people who might know about NZT.</i>

644
00:52:45,080 --> 00:52:47,460
<i>Vernon's other clients.</i>

645
00:53:27,250 --> 00:53:28,210
<i>- Hi.</i>
- Yeah, hi,

646
00:53:28,290 --> 00:53:29,750
may I please speak with Jerry Brady?

647
00:53:29,840 --> 00:53:32,210
<i>- Jerry's in the hospital.</i>
- He's what?

648
00:53:32,300 --> 00:53:34,170
<i>Yeah, he's not doing well at all.</i>

649
00:53:34,260 --> 00:53:39,100
- Yeah, hello, is Paul Kaplan there?
<i>- Uh, no. No, he's not.</i>

650
00:53:39,180 --> 00:53:42,470
<i>Uh, Paul passed away three days ago.</i>

651
00:53:43,600 --> 00:53:45,350
Oh, my God, I'm so sorry.

652
00:53:45,440 --> 00:53:48,020
<i>Yeah, he started getting headaches,
then he collapsed at work.</i>

653
00:53:48,110 --> 00:53:49,520
<i>Now he's in the ICU.</i>

654
00:53:49,610 --> 00:53:51,820
OK, please give him my best.
Thank you.

655
00:53:51,900 --> 00:53:57,280
<i>Of all the people I called...
three were dead and the rest were sick.</i>

656
00:54:20,220 --> 00:54:24,850
Hello? Hello?

657
00:54:43,040 --> 00:54:44,040
Watch it, man!

658
00:55:01,350 --> 00:55:02,430
Did you see that?

659
00:55:03,970 --> 00:55:04,970
Hey!

660
00:55:10,810 --> 00:55:14,230
Go! Go!

661
00:55:41,090 --> 00:55:42,390
Eddie.

662
00:55:44,430 --> 00:55:46,270
Melissa.

663
00:55:53,520 --> 00:55:54,820
Hey.

664
00:55:58,740 --> 00:56:02,030
Don't tell me I look good,
'cause I know.

665
00:56:03,990 --> 00:56:05,990
I didn't want you to see me this way.

666
00:56:06,620 --> 00:56:08,450
You look good.

667
00:56:08,540 --> 00:56:11,250
Oh, I, uh...

668
00:56:11,330 --> 00:56:13,380
Yeah, I sorta got myself together.

669
00:56:13,460 --> 00:56:15,920
NZT will do that to you.

670
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:18,920
- What do you mean?
- I mean, I took it, too.

671
00:56:19,880 --> 00:56:22,640
Vernon didn't tell you
any of this, did he?

672
00:56:22,720 --> 00:56:23,800
No.

673
00:56:23,890 --> 00:56:25,600
Well, when he told me
about this amazing new drug,

674
00:56:25,680 --> 00:56:29,640
I was like, down the hatch.
And it was. It was amazing.

675
00:56:29,730 --> 00:56:33,400
I read Brian Greene's
The Elegant Universe in 45 minutes

676
00:56:33,480 --> 00:56:35,310
and I understood it.

677
00:56:35,400 --> 00:56:37,690
My work rate increased
just insanely overnight

678
00:56:37,780 --> 00:56:39,490
and my boss started to hate me.

679
00:56:41,280 --> 00:56:46,160
They offered me his job and then...
and then I got scared.

680
00:56:47,080 --> 00:56:49,330
- Why?
- Why? Because I'm not stupid.

681
00:56:49,410 --> 00:56:52,670
Nobody can operate at that level
of mental activity and not crash.

682
00:56:55,500 --> 00:56:57,380
I stopped taking it.

683
00:56:57,460 --> 00:57:01,800
- And?
- And I got sick. Headaches.

684
00:57:01,880 --> 00:57:07,220
Throwing up.
So I went back to Vernon.

685
00:57:07,300 --> 00:57:09,810
He told me about all the people
that were dying.

686
00:57:11,100 --> 00:57:13,060
How much have you
been taking, Eddie?

687
00:57:13,140 --> 00:57:14,810
I've been taking a lot.

688
00:57:16,400 --> 00:57:20,820
Well, maybe... maybe they've
worked out the bugs. Maybe...

689
00:57:21,690 --> 00:57:23,280
Maybe this is a different batch.

690
00:57:24,700 --> 00:57:27,530
- Yeah.
- You're off it right now, aren't you?

691
00:57:27,620 --> 00:57:30,120
- Just finish your story.
- Well...

692
00:57:30,200 --> 00:57:33,040
...I didn't take any more
and I didn't die.

693
00:57:34,370 --> 00:57:37,460
But after a while, I realized I
couldn't concentrate on anything

694
00:57:37,540 --> 00:57:38,750
for longer than ten minutes.

695
00:57:38,840 --> 00:57:43,920
I, uh... I missed deadlines,
got lazy, slow, so slow.

696
00:57:44,630 --> 00:57:47,090
That was two years ago.

697
00:57:49,050 --> 00:57:51,100
And I haven't been the same since.

698
00:57:55,810 --> 00:57:59,690
Do you have some left? Good.

699
00:57:59,770 --> 00:58:03,150
You go home and you take it. You take
the dose down, but you don't just stop.

700
00:58:03,240 --> 00:58:04,990
You'll die if you just stop.

701
00:58:05,610 --> 00:58:10,410
Try to taper off, otherwise
the headaches are just the beginning.

702
00:58:12,580 --> 00:58:13,950
I have to go.

703
00:58:15,870 --> 00:58:18,420
- Do you know who makes it?
- I don't know.

704
00:58:20,000 --> 00:58:21,460
I have to go.

705
00:58:52,160 --> 00:58:56,960
Oh, shit. Listen, listen, listen.
Listen, listen, listen... OK.

706
00:58:57,040 --> 00:58:58,750
- You forget about me, huh?
- No, no, no, no.

707
00:58:58,830 --> 00:59:00,790
12:00 and you're not here.

708
00:59:00,880 --> 00:59:04,050
- I'm here now. Oh!
- Good.

709
00:59:04,130 --> 00:59:07,170
- Come on, get going.
- I could write you a check.

710
00:59:07,260 --> 00:59:12,220
Check. Check.
Are you out of your mind?

711
00:59:12,300 --> 00:59:14,560
What do you think I am?
Some financial institution?

712
00:59:14,640 --> 00:59:16,730
All right. You're right.
Look, we just need to go to a bank...

713
00:59:21,310 --> 00:59:23,690
What do you got there?

714
00:59:27,990 --> 00:59:30,410
- What is it?
- No, it's just aspirin.

715
00:59:30,490 --> 00:59:36,000
Don't look like no aspirin I ever see.
It's something good, huh?

716
00:59:54,760 --> 00:59:58,890
I feel good, man.
What's in this stuff?

717
00:59:58,980 --> 01:00:01,770
It's just, uh, vitamins and aspirin.

718
01:00:01,850 --> 01:00:05,570
You full of shit, Eddie baby.

719
01:00:31,050 --> 01:00:34,010
Lindy. I wouldn't
have let him in, but...

720
01:00:34,090 --> 01:00:36,850
I'm sorry... Tyler, I gotta go.

721
01:00:38,060 --> 01:00:39,730
I'm sick.

722
01:00:41,230 --> 01:00:45,110
I shouldn't... I'm sorry.
I couldn't make it home.

723
01:00:45,190 --> 01:00:48,280
- Let's get you to a hospital.
- No, no, no, no, no. I don't need...

724
01:00:48,360 --> 01:00:49,400
I need you to get...

725
01:00:49,490 --> 01:00:53,490
It's really simple, babe.
I need my pills.

726
01:00:53,570 --> 01:00:56,160
- Eddie, what pills?
- I don't... Babe, it's complicated.

727
01:00:56,240 --> 01:00:58,990
What are you taking?
Are you on drugs?

728
01:00:59,080 --> 01:01:02,000
They're... it's...

729
01:01:02,080 --> 01:01:05,420
<i>I had no choice.
I told Lindy everything.</i>

730
01:01:07,090 --> 01:01:11,510
So all this energy of yours,
all this focus...

731
01:01:11,590 --> 01:01:12,880
...has been a drug.

732
01:01:12,970 --> 01:01:15,340
Well, it was supposed to be legal.

733
01:01:15,430 --> 01:01:19,010
I have a... a supply stashed.

734
01:01:20,770 --> 01:01:22,520
You want me to go get it.

735
01:01:22,600 --> 01:01:26,060
- No. No, no, no. I'm gonna go get it.
- Give me your keys.

736
01:01:26,150 --> 01:01:27,820
I'll go to your apartment.

737
01:01:27,900 --> 01:01:31,780
It's not... in my apartment.
I moved it.

738
01:01:31,860 --> 01:01:35,660
OK, so where did you move it?

739
01:01:42,450 --> 01:01:44,330
In my house.

740
01:01:46,170 --> 01:01:47,630
Unbelievable.

741
01:01:59,890 --> 01:02:02,640
Hey, have you got it?

742
01:02:02,730 --> 01:02:04,310
Yeah.

743
01:02:06,350 --> 01:02:07,900
There's someone following me.

744
01:02:09,480 --> 01:02:10,690
Are you sure?

745
01:02:10,770 --> 01:02:13,240
Yeah. He got into the cab behind me

746
01:02:13,320 --> 01:02:15,320
and he's making every turn I'm making.

747
01:02:15,400 --> 01:02:19,240
- Baby, call the cops.
- Can you go around him?

748
01:02:19,330 --> 01:02:20,580
<i>Babe, what's happening?</i>

749
01:02:20,660 --> 01:02:22,450
We're stopped dead.

750
01:02:23,750 --> 01:02:26,120
He's getting out.

751
01:02:26,210 --> 01:02:28,750
Eddie, he's walking over here.

752
01:02:28,830 --> 01:02:29,840
Lindy?

753
01:02:30,750 --> 01:02:31,840
Oh, God!

754
01:02:33,670 --> 01:02:34,840
Ow!

755
01:02:46,940 --> 01:02:49,940
Help! Help!

756
01:02:51,520 --> 01:02:54,110
This man's...
This man's chasing me!

757
01:02:54,190 --> 01:02:57,740
He followed me from
my building. I don't know him.

758
01:02:57,820 --> 01:03:01,200
- Please!
- Yo, buddy, let's leave her alone!

759
01:03:03,700 --> 01:03:05,870
Are you deaf?
I said leave her alone.

760
01:03:07,960 --> 01:03:10,080
Hey, buddy, are you
trying to piss me off?

761
01:03:11,880 --> 01:03:14,130
What are you doing, man?

762
01:03:45,660 --> 01:03:48,580
- Are you still there?
- Yeah, I'm here. What happened?

763
01:03:48,660 --> 01:03:51,830
I'm stuck. He'll find me.

764
01:03:51,920 --> 01:03:53,920
<i>OK, stop talking. Don't move.</i>

765
01:03:54,000 --> 01:03:56,670
<i>- He killed two guys.
- What?</i>

766
01:03:56,760 --> 01:03:59,930
- He's gonna kill me, too.
- No, no, no...

767
01:04:00,010 --> 01:04:01,180
Can he see you?

768
01:04:04,850 --> 01:04:06,390
No.

769
01:04:07,600 --> 01:04:08,980
Not yet.

770
01:04:10,350 --> 01:04:12,900
I don't know what to do.

771
01:04:12,980 --> 01:04:16,690
OK, I need you to go into the bag
and take one of the pills.

772
01:04:17,530 --> 01:04:18,780
Why?

773
01:04:18,860 --> 01:04:21,280
<i>Because you'll know what to do.
You'll take it and then you'll know.</i>

774
01:04:21,360 --> 01:04:25,280
He's got a knife. Eddie, I can't
think my way out of a knife.

775
01:04:26,490 --> 01:04:28,580
Just listen to me.
It'll come on in 30 seconds.

776
01:04:28,660 --> 01:04:30,540
And you'll think your way out.
That's what it does.

777
01:04:54,060 --> 01:04:57,650
He's getting closer.

778
01:05:00,280 --> 01:05:01,780
Lindy, I love you.

779
01:05:33,480 --> 01:05:35,400
Hey!

780
01:06:13,100 --> 01:06:14,690
Now turn to me...

781
01:06:16,520 --> 01:06:18,230
Hey, hey, hey, hey!

782
01:06:20,020 --> 01:06:22,610
- Ah! - Daddy!

783
01:06:22,690 --> 01:06:24,240
Ah!

784
01:06:32,950 --> 01:06:34,540
Eddie. Here.

785
01:06:35,660 --> 01:06:37,750
It's gonna be OK.

786
01:06:40,040 --> 01:06:43,550
We've got to get out of here. Eddie?

787
01:07:17,670 --> 01:07:19,040
You OK?

788
01:07:20,500 --> 01:07:22,290
Yeah.

789
01:07:24,170 --> 01:07:27,510
I'm so sorry about what happened.

790
01:07:27,590 --> 01:07:30,680
I want you to know that you never,
ever have to go through that again,

791
01:07:30,760 --> 01:07:33,510
'cause I can take care of us now.

792
01:07:33,600 --> 01:07:35,640
I'm sure you can.

793
01:07:35,720 --> 01:07:38,190
I can make us safe.
I'm back, OK?

794
01:07:42,520 --> 01:07:44,820
- Who's back, Eddie?
- Me.

795
01:07:44,900 --> 01:07:47,320
No. It's not you.

796
01:07:47,400 --> 01:07:50,990
- You were different before.
- I'm still the same person, Lin.

797
01:07:51,070 --> 01:07:52,700
I wasn't, when I was on it.

798
01:07:53,910 --> 01:07:56,040
I did things I would never do.

799
01:07:56,120 --> 01:07:58,710
- Those things saved your life.
- But they weren't me.

800
01:07:58,790 --> 01:08:00,330
Yes, they were.
No, the way it works...

801
01:08:00,420 --> 01:08:02,880
I know how it works. I get it.

802
01:08:02,960 --> 01:08:05,750
I totally get it.
You feel invincible.

803
01:08:07,010 --> 01:08:09,680
Where it will take us, Lin,
you can't even imagine.

804
01:08:09,760 --> 01:08:14,180
The things we'll be able to do.
Where we'll be a year from now.

805
01:08:14,260 --> 01:08:17,930
The kind of life that
we'll finally be able to live.

806
01:08:18,020 --> 01:08:21,230
Well, you might have
to live it without me.

807
01:08:25,270 --> 01:08:27,900
Look, I am gonna stop taking it.
There's just some things I have to do

808
01:08:27,990 --> 01:08:31,530
to keep us safe and that... To set us up
and then I'm gonna get off it.

809
01:08:31,610 --> 01:08:33,200
- I mean, that's the plan.
- Eddie...

810
01:08:33,280 --> 01:08:35,580
Look, I have a plan. I swear.

811
01:08:35,660 --> 01:08:37,450
I'm gonna get off it.

812
01:08:38,910 --> 01:08:40,250
OK.

813
01:08:42,210 --> 01:08:43,790
I hope you do.

814
01:09:15,990 --> 01:09:17,870
Looking for me?

815
01:09:20,250 --> 01:09:23,920
You think you can run out on me, huh?
You think I don't know where you are?

816
01:09:24,000 --> 01:09:25,750
I was under the impression
our business was settled.

817
01:09:25,830 --> 01:09:27,630
I want some more of that stuff.

818
01:09:27,710 --> 01:09:30,670
Or I shoot you right here.
Take your pick.

819
01:09:30,760 --> 01:09:32,550
<i>This guy was not going away.</i>

820
01:09:32,630 --> 01:09:34,390
<i>A few pills would buy me some time.</i>

821
01:09:34,470 --> 01:09:37,850
- There it is. We're done.
- Oh, yeah. We'll see.

822
01:09:37,930 --> 01:09:40,470
<i>Until I found a more...
permanent solution.</i>

823
01:09:41,310 --> 01:09:45,060
Unless you see a real threat, I never
want it known that I have any security.

824
01:09:45,150 --> 01:09:47,360
You'll never precede me,
you'll always follow me.

825
01:09:47,440 --> 01:09:50,900
Never more than 15 steps behind.
And never stand too close together.

826
01:09:50,990 --> 01:09:53,700
I want to able to look behind me
if the opportunity presents itself.

827
01:09:55,110 --> 01:09:57,370
And don't wear the same color suit.
This isn't The Matrix.

828
01:10:03,120 --> 01:10:05,290
You have to know, Eddie,
I wrote you off.

829
01:10:05,370 --> 01:10:07,000
- I was sick.
- You don't get sick.

830
01:10:07,080 --> 01:10:09,800
You're playing at this level, you get
hit by a car, you don't even die.

831
01:10:09,880 --> 01:10:12,550
- You're absolutely right.
- I know I'm right.

832
01:10:12,630 --> 01:10:14,510
I mean, I didn't know who or what
I was talking to.

833
01:10:14,590 --> 01:10:17,800
A hundred and five. That's who
you were talking to, Carl. Delirium.

834
01:10:17,890 --> 01:10:20,430
Mmm-hm.

835
01:10:20,510 --> 01:10:22,220
I sent over my revised projections.

836
01:10:22,310 --> 01:10:23,230
I didn't ask for your projections.

837
01:10:23,850 --> 01:10:26,400
I know, but I think if
you take a look at them...

838
01:10:26,480 --> 01:10:28,480
I already have.
As a matter of fact...

839
01:10:30,020 --> 01:10:33,240
...I fired a few people
over your projections.

840
01:10:33,320 --> 01:10:34,950
- I'm sorry.
- No, you're not.

841
01:10:35,030 --> 01:10:37,820
You're relieved that you know things
my team missed.

842
01:10:37,910 --> 01:10:42,450
The minute,
the instant that's no longer true...

843
01:10:45,370 --> 01:10:46,960
<i>Two months after I'd started NZT,</i>

844
01:10:47,040 --> 01:10:50,130
<i>I was brokering the biggest merger
in corporate history.</i>

845
01:10:50,210 --> 01:10:54,630
Ironically, it's gonna be crude. Crude
is gonna be where we're gonna hit them.

846
01:10:54,720 --> 01:10:57,340
Which yields a prospective profit
of 200 percent.

847
01:10:57,430 --> 01:10:59,300
That makes both of us
very happy campers.

848
01:10:59,390 --> 01:11:01,760
<i>I found that if
I maintained an even dose,</i>

849
01:11:01,850 --> 01:11:04,180
<i>remembered to eat,
drank no alcohol,</i>

850
01:11:04,270 --> 01:11:06,690
<i>- the blackouts didn't recur.</i>
- How you doing, Eddie?

851
01:11:07,650 --> 01:11:10,610
Tom, is it possible to construct
an imperceptible compartment?

852
01:11:10,690 --> 01:11:12,150
In this jacket?

853
01:11:12,230 --> 01:11:15,650
- In all of them.
- Certainly, sir.

854
01:11:15,740 --> 01:11:19,820
<i>Safer though my stash now was, I
was not sitting around until it ran out.</i>

855
01:11:19,910 --> 01:11:23,240
Can I combine these ingredients
in the same exact quantity, yes, but,

856
01:11:23,330 --> 01:11:28,120
the exact method of delivery
to the brain... Darts at a dartboard.

857
01:11:28,210 --> 01:11:30,830
- Meaning?
- Clinical trials, guinea pig people.

858
01:11:30,920 --> 01:11:33,420
- That's gonna take too long.
- It's what it is.

859
01:11:33,500 --> 01:11:37,010
Or you'll kill people.
You need 12, to 18 months...

860
01:11:37,090 --> 01:11:39,220
I'll give you two million dollars
if you can do it in six.

861
01:11:47,770 --> 01:11:51,690
I found that we can cut costs
30 percent and, um...

862
01:11:55,110 --> 01:11:56,440
Excuse me one second.

863
01:11:59,950 --> 01:12:02,160
I thought we straightened this out,
Detective.

864
01:12:02,240 --> 01:12:06,160
This isn't about Vernon Gant,
Mr. Morra.

865
01:12:06,250 --> 01:12:08,620
What is this about?

866
01:12:08,710 --> 01:12:14,040
The witness identified the Maria Winberg
suspect as this person.

867
01:12:18,720 --> 01:12:21,640
Wanna tell me your whereabouts
the night of April 2nd?

868
01:12:23,600 --> 01:12:25,810
<i>I couldn't keep
this quiet on my own.</i>

869
01:12:26,970 --> 01:12:31,480
<i>Enter Morris Brandt...
the most lethal lawyer in New York.</i>

870
01:12:34,480 --> 01:12:39,150
You're lucky somebody wiped the room.
It's weak. Circumstantial at best.

871
01:12:39,240 --> 01:12:42,240
Just between us, were you there?

872
01:12:42,320 --> 01:12:43,570
I don't remember.

873
01:12:45,200 --> 01:12:46,700
I don't want to
make concessions to you...

874
01:12:46,790 --> 01:12:48,830
<i>Carl Van Loon and Hank Atwood</i>

875
01:12:48,910 --> 01:12:52,920
<i>had no idea I might
soon be charged with murder.</i>

876
01:12:53,000 --> 01:12:55,670
But SyCorps, Andine
and others are jockeying

877
01:12:55,750 --> 01:12:57,760
for the same industrial
concessions in Libya

878
01:12:57,840 --> 01:13:00,090
that both our companies are
pretending we're not sniffing out.

879
01:13:00,170 --> 01:13:01,550
How do you know this?

880
01:13:02,550 --> 01:13:05,220
The governmental bribe system
is itself corrupt, so, of course,

881
01:13:05,300 --> 01:13:08,430
information about its inner workings,
like anything, can be bought.

882
01:13:11,020 --> 01:13:12,390
Go on.

883
01:13:12,480 --> 01:13:15,860
If you take a look at the lower
left-hand column, in 2008,

884
01:13:15,940 --> 01:13:19,360
five companies were jockeying for a
position before we even took notice.

885
01:13:22,740 --> 01:13:24,450
Jesus, he looks frail.

886
01:13:25,490 --> 01:13:27,870
- Could be an act.
- He's not even 60.

887
01:13:30,200 --> 01:13:33,250
So, have you decided, Eddie?
What are you gonna do?

888
01:13:33,330 --> 01:13:36,420
When this is over? I don't know.

889
01:13:36,500 --> 01:13:37,880
You haven't given me the answer.

890
01:13:37,960 --> 01:13:39,800
You haven't asked the question.

891
01:13:39,880 --> 01:13:41,630
Well, given the scale
of my contribution,

892
01:13:41,720 --> 01:13:44,050
let's say 45.

893
01:13:44,130 --> 01:13:47,140
Done. Forty-five thousand dollars.

894
01:13:47,220 --> 01:13:50,640
Forty million is plenty, Eddie.
There's a lot more where that came from.

895
01:13:50,720 --> 01:13:53,020
You know, Eddie...

896
01:13:53,100 --> 01:13:54,140
...it may be on your mind

897
01:13:54,230 --> 01:13:56,650
that you're not gonna
continue to work for me any more.

898
01:13:56,730 --> 01:13:58,440
This has been
the learning experience of my life.

899
01:13:58,520 --> 01:14:00,990
I hope it has. I hope you don't
think you've gotten enough from me.

900
01:14:01,070 --> 01:14:02,610
On to the next.

901
01:14:02,700 --> 01:14:05,450
Well, in order for a career to evolve,
I'm gonna have to move on.

902
01:14:05,530 --> 01:14:07,990
That you would even think that
would only show me

903
01:14:08,080 --> 01:14:10,490
how unprepared you are
to be on your own.

904
01:14:10,580 --> 01:14:12,960
I mean, you do know you're a freak.

905
01:14:13,040 --> 01:14:15,540
Your deductive powers are a gift
from God, or chance,

906
01:14:15,620 --> 01:14:17,960
or a stray shot of sperm,
or whatever, or whoever the hell wrote

907
01:14:18,040 --> 01:14:20,340
your life script, a gift not earned.

908
01:14:20,420 --> 01:14:24,300
You do not know what I know because
you have not earned those powers.

909
01:14:24,380 --> 01:14:26,090
You're careless with those powers.

910
01:14:26,180 --> 01:14:29,470
You flaunt them and you throw them
around like a brat with his trust fund.

911
01:14:29,560 --> 01:14:32,520
You haven't had to climb up
all the greasy little rungs.

912
01:14:32,600 --> 01:14:35,560
You haven't been bored blind at the
fundraisers. You haven't done the time

913
01:14:35,640 --> 01:14:37,480
in that first marriage
to the girl with the right father.

914
01:14:37,560 --> 01:14:40,270
You think you can leap
over all in a single bound.

915
01:14:40,360 --> 01:14:42,860
You haven't had to bribe
or charm or threaten your way

916
01:14:42,940 --> 01:14:46,160
to a seat at that table. You don't
know how to assess your competition

917
01:14:46,240 --> 01:14:49,450
because you haven't competed.
Don't make me your competition.

918
01:14:52,290 --> 01:14:54,330
Now, I'll open up
a line of credit for you.

919
01:14:54,410 --> 01:14:56,460
You'll be wanting a few toys.

920
01:15:14,140 --> 01:15:16,810
<i>Hotel rooms
were no longer an option.</i>

921
01:15:16,890 --> 01:15:18,900
<i>What I needed was a bunker.</i>

922
01:15:18,980 --> 01:15:21,400
<i>There's a built-in safe,
central surveillance,</i>

923
01:15:21,480 --> 01:15:24,070
<i>vault grade pneumatic doors.</i>

924
01:15:24,150 --> 01:15:27,450
It's an unparalleled
three-tier security system.

925
01:15:27,530 --> 01:15:30,910
A fortress with a hell of a view.

926
01:15:35,160 --> 01:15:37,920
- What's the asking price?
- Eight-point-five.

927
01:15:42,920 --> 01:15:44,300
I'll take it.

928
01:15:49,930 --> 01:15:52,760
<i>Right away, it was obvious
he was on NZT.</i>

929
01:15:52,850 --> 01:15:55,520
Next week, I'm gonna need 20 pills.

930
01:15:55,600 --> 01:15:57,560
Next week, you can go fuck yourself!

931
01:15:57,640 --> 01:15:59,230
Not that you'd feel anything.

932
01:16:00,190 --> 01:16:03,860
I don't think your Fortune 500
financial friends would appreciate

933
01:16:03,940 --> 01:16:07,070
the details of your little dilemma
with the police.

934
01:16:08,030 --> 01:16:10,780
Thursday, I'm gonna need the pills here.

935
01:16:13,830 --> 01:16:15,240
Getting smarter!

936
01:16:17,120 --> 01:16:19,120
<i>Obviously, this could not go on,</i>

937
01:16:19,210 --> 01:16:22,380
<i>but there is very little on this earth
that 40 million dollars can't solve.</i>

938
01:16:22,460 --> 01:16:26,090
<i>And tomorrow at nine,
Atwood would sign the papers.</i>

939
01:16:33,430 --> 01:16:36,430
- Do you want to call it?
- Cold feet?

940
01:16:36,520 --> 01:16:39,100
There was all last night
to tell us that.

941
01:16:40,640 --> 01:16:43,900
- Anything?
<i>- I'll call him again, sir.</i>

942
01:16:43,980 --> 01:16:46,190
OK, the decision could
have changed. That makes sense.

943
01:16:46,280 --> 01:16:49,650
The discourtesy doesn't.
It's still on.

944
01:16:50,450 --> 01:16:52,660
Oh, based on your charts
and projections.

945
01:16:53,620 --> 01:16:55,830
You know, remind me,
who the fuck are you, anyway?

946
01:16:55,910 --> 01:16:58,620
I'm sorry, Carl, I've had it with this
unqualified, posturing little prick.

947
01:16:58,700 --> 01:17:00,830
- Pull it back.
- Since when is this the Delphic Oracle?

948
01:17:00,920 --> 01:17:04,750
- Pull it back.
- Excuse me, Mr. Van Loon?

949
01:17:04,840 --> 01:17:06,300
Mrs. Atwood is in your office.

950
01:17:09,220 --> 01:17:11,630
Ah. Mrs. Atwood, Carl Van Loon.

951
01:17:11,720 --> 01:17:14,140
- My associate, Edward Morra.
- Pleasure.

952
01:17:16,600 --> 01:17:19,180
I wanted to make this
as confidential as possible.

953
01:17:19,270 --> 01:17:20,850
Of course.

954
01:17:20,940 --> 01:17:24,860
My husband experienced some pain
and dizziness this morning.

955
01:17:24,940 --> 01:17:27,480
He's currently at Lenox Hill,
undergoing tests.

956
01:17:28,690 --> 01:17:30,650
I just want you to know
we have every intention

957
01:17:30,740 --> 01:17:32,450
of signing the contract.

958
01:17:33,280 --> 01:17:36,490
And as soon as he is able,
we will proceed.

959
01:17:37,660 --> 01:17:40,580
I realize this is a useless platitude,
but if there's anything that we can do,

960
01:17:40,660 --> 01:17:42,000
please don't hesitate to tell us.

961
01:17:42,080 --> 01:17:43,920
Obviously, we want to be
as helpful and respectful

962
01:17:44,000 --> 01:17:45,540
as possible, Mrs. Atwood.

963
01:17:45,630 --> 01:17:48,420
Thank you, gentlemen.
I rely on your discretion.

964
01:17:50,460 --> 01:17:51,510
There won't be a proxy.

965
01:17:51,590 --> 01:17:54,640
No, a cagey prick like him
giving away power of attorney?

966
01:17:59,100 --> 01:18:01,100
Well, he'd better get better.

967
01:18:04,230 --> 01:18:08,650
<i>But Atwood wouldn't get better.
Because Atwood was out of NZT.</i>

968
01:18:12,150 --> 01:18:15,700
Well, why be surprised?

969
01:18:15,780 --> 01:18:19,200
<i>How many other meteoric rises
might be explained by NZT?</i>

970
01:18:19,290 --> 01:18:20,700
<i>At least I had some.</i>

971
01:18:20,790 --> 01:18:22,910
<i>- My life wasn't in jeopardy.
- You can wait here.</i>

972
01:18:23,000 --> 01:18:26,000
<i>Only my money. And my liberty.</i>

973
01:18:27,420 --> 01:18:28,790
I've been all over with them.

974
01:18:28,880 --> 01:18:31,260
We can't allow
any disparity in race

975
01:18:31,340 --> 01:18:34,090
or physical type between you
and the rest of the line up.

976
01:18:34,170 --> 01:18:35,130
OK.

977
01:18:35,220 --> 01:18:37,600
They're as close to your clones
as I could possibly get.

978
01:18:37,680 --> 01:18:41,430
<i>It's gotta be just one big
handsome blur to this guy.</i>

979
01:18:55,200 --> 01:18:57,110
<i>Who was out there?</i>

980
01:18:58,870 --> 01:19:00,740
<i>The woman's husband?</i>

981
01:19:01,540 --> 01:19:03,450
<i>A hotel maid?</i>

982
01:19:05,410 --> 01:19:07,920
<i>Was this how it was all going to end?</i>

983
01:19:12,250 --> 01:19:14,800
Not the dimmest clue.
He was dithering.

984
01:19:14,880 --> 01:19:17,800
"He was the third one from the right.
No. Not him."

985
01:19:17,890 --> 01:19:23,310
There you go. It's a thing of beauty.
You have that made?

986
01:19:23,390 --> 01:19:24,980
I did.

987
01:19:41,830 --> 01:19:43,660
<i>...market's been mixed today.</i>

988
01:19:43,740 --> 01:19:46,460
<i>The Dow has been yo-yoing
amidst speculation that.</i>

989
01:19:46,540 --> 01:19:51,250
<i>Van Loon Associates and Hank Atwood
have negotiated a merger...</i>

990
01:19:52,380 --> 01:19:54,130
Have you been talking to anyone?

991
01:19:55,550 --> 01:19:58,340
No, I haven't been talking to anybody,
Carl. I'm not stupid.

992
01:19:58,430 --> 01:19:59,680
I know you're not stupid, Eddie,

993
01:19:59,760 --> 01:20:01,680
but don't make the classic
smart person's mistake.

994
01:20:01,760 --> 01:20:03,640
Thinking no one's smarter than you.

995
01:20:03,720 --> 01:20:06,850
Where the hell have you been? This is
the second time you've turned to vapor.

996
01:20:06,930 --> 01:20:09,810
I was in the building, I was getting
coffee. Why, has there been movement?

997
01:20:09,900 --> 01:20:12,190
Yeah, there's been movement alright.
Atwood's in a coma.

998
01:20:12,270 --> 01:20:14,820
He wasn't playing us.
He's dying.

999
01:20:14,900 --> 01:20:16,490
What do you know about this?

1000
01:20:16,570 --> 01:20:20,160
<i>I was less than
usually equipped to answer.</i>

1001
01:20:20,240 --> 01:20:24,580
<i>The dullness was coming on.
I was overdue to take my NZT.</i>

1002
01:20:24,660 --> 01:20:27,870
<i>And the pain was
starting behind my eyes.</i>

1003
01:20:27,960 --> 01:20:29,210
Excuse me.

1004
01:20:29,290 --> 01:20:30,790
You're not excused.

1005
01:20:30,880 --> 01:20:33,420
What? A man in your employ
can't take a piss, Carl?

1006
01:20:56,110 --> 01:20:57,990
<i>Was it Brandt or someone else?</i>

1007
01:20:58,070 --> 01:21:00,650
<i>The police station?
A coatroom?</i>

1008
01:21:14,670 --> 01:21:15,920
Mr. Morra.

1009
01:21:16,000 --> 01:21:18,510
- Carl, I'm sorry...
- Mr. Morra. Mr. Morra!

1010
01:21:18,590 --> 01:21:20,340
- This just came for you.
- Yeah, what?

1011
01:21:24,930 --> 01:21:28,850
Hope not, so let me know
when you know.

1012
01:21:29,560 --> 01:21:31,520
All right, I'm gonna ask you again,
Eddie. What do you know about this?

1013
01:21:31,600 --> 01:21:34,560
- What? About the leak?
- No. About Atwood.

1014
01:21:34,650 --> 01:21:36,480
What? What about Atwood?

1015
01:21:36,570 --> 01:21:38,820
Look, you got a long way to go
as a liar, kiddo.

1016
01:21:38,900 --> 01:21:40,110
- Carl, I don't know what you're...
- Listen!

1017
01:21:40,190 --> 01:21:41,570
What you're about to say is bullshit.

1018
01:21:41,650 --> 01:21:43,160
I know there's something
you're hiding from me.

1019
01:21:43,240 --> 01:21:45,120
I smell it on you. Right now,

1020
01:21:45,200 --> 01:21:48,580
I wanna know why this clusterfuck
of a deal is dead in the water!

1021
01:21:50,250 --> 01:21:52,290
Carl.

1022
01:21:52,370 --> 01:21:54,380
Eddie! Eddie!

1023
01:22:10,980 --> 01:22:12,850
<i>Outside Lenox Hill Hospital today,</i>

1024
01:22:12,940 --> 01:22:15,690
<i>more twists and turns
in the Van Loon-Atwood merger.</i>

1025
01:22:15,770 --> 01:22:19,570
<i>There is absolutely no truth
to the rumor of this merger.</i>

1026
01:22:19,650 --> 01:22:23,360
<i>None whatsoever.
My husband is having tests.</i>

1027
01:22:23,450 --> 01:22:25,530
<i>This is a difficult time.</i>

1028
01:22:25,610 --> 01:22:29,540
<i>We would appreciate your directing
further questions to our attorney.</i>

1029
01:22:29,620 --> 01:22:32,040
<i>Yes, we have no further comments
at this time.</i>

1030
01:22:32,120 --> 01:22:34,540
<i>We're just awaiting the results
of the latest round of tests</i>

1031
01:22:34,620 --> 01:22:35,580
<i>and hoping for the best.</i>

1032
01:22:35,670 --> 01:22:37,080
<i>Thank you.</i>

1033
01:23:16,000 --> 01:23:20,500
Eddie.
I know you're in there, Eddie.

1034
01:23:21,800 --> 01:23:24,670
Open up.

1035
01:23:26,930 --> 01:23:29,010
Come on.

1036
01:23:31,260 --> 01:23:33,140
Open the door.

1037
01:23:49,870 --> 01:23:52,580
<i>And so, here I am.</i>

1038
01:23:55,370 --> 01:23:58,040
<i>I knew they would kill me
slowly and horribly.</i>

1039
01:23:58,120 --> 01:24:01,250
<i>But this choice
would at least be mine.</i>

1040
01:24:10,680 --> 01:24:15,020
<i>But we're instinctive creatures.
We want to live.</i>

1041
01:24:15,100 --> 01:24:19,600
<i>So my foggy brain tried to remember
where one tablet of NZT might be.</i>

1042
01:24:26,900 --> 01:24:31,660
<i>It was possible.
And possible was enough.</i>

1043
01:24:55,010 --> 01:24:56,600
Hey, Eddie.

1044
01:25:19,410 --> 01:25:22,370
Now you know I want more,
so why would you upset me?

1045
01:25:23,710 --> 01:25:26,300
I don't like being down
to this last one.

1046
01:25:26,380 --> 01:25:28,300
You don't even know how to use it.

1047
01:25:28,380 --> 01:25:30,720
You don't appreciate it.

1048
01:25:30,800 --> 01:25:33,890
You see, I dissolve in a solution.

1049
01:25:33,970 --> 01:25:38,640
You shoot it. It goes straight
into the blood and brain.

1050
01:25:40,270 --> 01:25:42,440
Lasts much longer.

1051
01:25:57,280 --> 01:25:58,870
So...

1052
01:25:58,950 --> 01:26:01,580
You gonna tell me where the rest is?

1053
01:26:01,660 --> 01:26:03,330
I'm plumb out.

1054
01:26:03,830 --> 01:26:05,960
(Search!)

1055
01:26:07,960 --> 01:26:10,670
You know, these guys are gonna find it.

1056
01:26:11,840 --> 01:26:14,390
Or I'm gonna make you tell me.

1057
01:26:14,470 --> 01:26:17,430
Question is, which is gonna come first?

1058
01:26:18,390 --> 01:26:20,520
I guess we're gonna find out, huh?

1059
01:26:24,190 --> 01:26:27,480
This is a waste of my time,
you know?

1060
01:26:27,570 --> 01:26:30,480
I'm getting out
of this side of the business.

1061
01:26:30,570 --> 01:26:35,280
I'm branching out.
I'm starting import-export company.

1062
01:26:36,160 --> 01:26:37,280
Very exciting stuff.

1063
01:26:37,370 --> 01:26:41,000
I got a lot on my plate.
You're lucky I'm here.

1064
01:26:46,250 --> 01:26:48,170
(There is a safe.)

1065
01:26:48,920 --> 01:26:54,510
Back in the old times, I might
peel your skin back. Fillet you.

1066
01:26:54,590 --> 01:26:57,340
"Fillet." It's a nice word.

1067
01:26:57,430 --> 01:27:02,140
I didn't know this before.
Oh, I had such a poor vocabulary.

1068
01:27:07,150 --> 01:27:09,400
But then I discovered
this thing, Google.

1069
01:27:09,480 --> 01:27:13,530
Did you know the small and large
intestines is 20 foot long?

1070
01:27:13,610 --> 01:27:15,450
I had no idea.
I don't believe it.

1071
01:27:15,530 --> 01:27:19,330
I gotta see this for myself.
I'm dubious. It's another nice word.

1072
01:27:19,410 --> 01:27:23,750
Anyway, I'm sure you're curious, too.

1073
01:27:23,830 --> 01:27:26,330
Don't worry, everything's sterilized.

1074
01:27:26,420 --> 01:27:29,460
We're gonna keep you alive
for the whole shebang.

1075
01:27:29,540 --> 01:27:31,710
You're gonna get
a front row seat, Eddie.

1076
01:27:34,170 --> 01:27:36,050
What?

1077
01:27:38,390 --> 01:27:40,640
Ah!

1078
01:28:09,120 --> 01:28:11,290
<i>Not much of a triumph, was it?</i>

1079
01:28:11,380 --> 01:28:13,590
<i>Because I would die here, too.</i>

1080
01:28:15,710 --> 01:28:20,010
<i>Only NZT could help me and the last
of it was in this fuck's bloodstream.</i>

1081
01:29:35,710 --> 01:29:36,960
Victor!

1082
01:29:44,720 --> 01:29:46,350
What?

1083
01:29:53,980 --> 01:29:54,900
Ah!

1084
01:30:00,900 --> 01:30:02,610
(I can't see.)

1085
01:30:03,660 --> 01:30:04,410
Ah!

1086
01:30:13,460 --> 01:30:15,290
Ah!

1087
01:30:45,450 --> 01:30:47,160
(To your right, shoot!)

1088
01:30:55,750 --> 01:30:56,750
Victor?

1089
01:31:00,050 --> 01:31:01,260
Victor?

1090
01:31:02,420 --> 01:31:03,760
Victor?

1091
01:31:53,390 --> 01:31:54,890
<i>The police would later note</i>

1092
01:31:54,980 --> 01:31:57,600
<i>that my apartment's previous
owner was an arms dealer.</i>

1093
01:31:57,690 --> 01:32:02,860
<i>Of course, these intruders had
been his dissatisfied clients.</i>

1094
01:32:02,940 --> 01:32:05,570
<i>And the night was still young.</i>

1095
01:32:18,040 --> 01:32:20,170
I'm sure you're curious
about what happened to your boss.

1096
01:32:20,250 --> 01:32:22,380
I know I certainly am.

1097
01:32:22,460 --> 01:32:25,300
I mean, if my attorney was
really working for Mr. Atwood,

1098
01:32:25,380 --> 01:32:29,640
trying to save his life,
why is he dead?

1099
01:32:29,720 --> 01:32:32,300
I mean, at what point was
the pony express supposed to ride in

1100
01:32:32,390 --> 01:32:34,470
with his life-saving medication?

1101
01:32:35,520 --> 01:32:37,560
<i>His boss was dead.</i>

1102
01:32:37,640 --> 01:32:41,730
<i>So, I didn't matter now.</i>

1103
01:32:41,810 --> 01:32:46,530
<i>But he did not appreciate that Morris
Brandt had kept the pills for himself.</i>

1104
01:32:58,500 --> 01:33:00,830
<i>And it was all still possible.</i>

1105
01:33:03,340 --> 01:33:05,880
(TWELVE MONTHS LATER)

1106
01:33:19,310 --> 01:33:21,810
Actually, no, there's no more tickets.

1107
01:33:21,900 --> 01:33:26,360
Yeah, I know. An overbooked fundraiser.
That's a first.

1108
01:33:26,440 --> 01:33:28,110
Well, here he comes. I'll ask him.

1109
01:33:28,860 --> 01:33:30,280
Oh, that's much better.

1110
01:33:30,360 --> 01:33:32,910
- Great job.
- Thank you, sir.

1111
01:33:32,990 --> 01:33:34,120
Hi, Mr. Morra.

1112
01:33:34,200 --> 01:33:36,040
Edward!

1113
01:33:37,410 --> 01:33:39,660
I think we're looking
at a second overflow event.

1114
01:33:39,750 --> 01:33:41,750
There's a wave of donations coming in
and I don't think

1115
01:33:41,830 --> 01:33:44,130
they're even gonna
try a second debate.

1116
01:33:44,210 --> 01:33:45,460
- Great. - Hold on.

1117
01:33:45,540 --> 01:33:47,170
A guy from
Eiben-Chemcorp's in your office.

1118
01:33:47,250 --> 01:33:49,010
- I have a lunch.
- They're your biggest contributor.

1119
01:33:49,090 --> 01:33:50,300
Give him his two crappy little minutes.

1120
01:33:50,380 --> 01:33:52,760
- Eiben-Chemcorp Pharmaceuticals?
- Yeah.

1121
01:33:54,640 --> 01:33:57,350
Finally get to meet the guys
that keep the lights on.

1122
01:33:58,850 --> 01:34:00,640
- Carl!
- Mr. Morra.

1123
01:34:02,100 --> 01:34:05,610
What a surprise. They told me somebody
from Eiben was coming.

1124
01:34:05,690 --> 01:34:09,030
Chemcorp. Yeah, that's me now. I'm
moving out of the energy business.

1125
01:34:09,110 --> 01:34:12,820
Come on. That's like Hef
moving out of the mansion.

1126
01:34:12,910 --> 01:34:16,160
Yeah. I bought Eiben a couple of months
ago. I'm surprised you didn't hear.

1127
01:34:16,240 --> 01:34:20,290
No. No. Well, they really
keep me hopping here.

1128
01:34:20,370 --> 01:34:23,170
Well, it's gonna pay off,
Senator Morra.

1129
01:34:24,500 --> 01:34:26,880
The Senate. Who knows...

1130
01:34:26,960 --> 01:34:30,300
Everything's a technicality
from here on in, isn't it, Eddie?

1131
01:34:30,970 --> 01:34:35,550
I think we both know where it's headed,
if you can maintain.

1132
01:34:38,390 --> 01:34:41,600
Have you asked yourself what
you're gonna do when you run out?

1133
01:34:57,280 --> 01:35:00,290
- When did you know?
- Does it matter? I know now.

1134
01:35:01,370 --> 01:35:04,790
I wish you'd come to me at first, Eddie.
I could have bought Eiben at 33 a share.

1135
01:35:04,870 --> 01:35:06,290
We could have been partners.

1136
01:35:10,050 --> 01:35:11,880
So, Eiben makes NZT.

1137
01:35:11,920 --> 01:35:14,760
Well, it'll never officially exist.

1138
01:35:14,840 --> 01:35:17,390
Obviously, I don't need
to point out the advantages for you.

1139
01:35:18,300 --> 01:35:19,810
Oh, I think maybe you do.

1140
01:35:19,890 --> 01:35:23,810
Limitless supply.
We know you can't get any more.

1141
01:35:23,890 --> 01:35:25,390
You sure about that?

1142
01:35:25,480 --> 01:35:27,310
They shut down your lab this morning.

1143
01:35:29,820 --> 01:35:33,360
Eddie. I just want to help you,
that's all.

1144
01:35:33,440 --> 01:35:36,410
Keep you supplied.
No disruption in service.

1145
01:35:36,490 --> 01:35:38,570
Give you peace of mind.
That's all.

1146
01:35:40,240 --> 01:35:43,250
And in return, I do what?

1147
01:35:43,330 --> 01:35:47,120
Get a little advice, maybe.
I won't be bothering you much at first.

1148
01:35:47,210 --> 01:35:49,670
And later. What are you
gonna be bothering me with?

1149
01:35:49,750 --> 01:35:53,420
Well, given where you're heading,
some of our ideas are a bit ambitious.

1150
01:35:53,510 --> 01:35:55,380
But hey, I think they're achievable.

1151
01:35:55,470 --> 01:35:57,220
And what if I don't like your ideas?

1152
01:35:57,300 --> 01:35:59,680
Then we'll say, "Godspeed."

1153
01:35:59,760 --> 01:36:03,270
And your candle will have shed a brief,
but lovely light.

1154
01:36:05,140 --> 01:36:06,600
But you know what, Eddie,
let's not even

1155
01:36:06,690 --> 01:36:07,690
go there right now.

1156
01:36:07,770 --> 01:36:11,070
Right now, I just want
to keep you brilliant...

1157
01:36:11,150 --> 01:36:14,860
...and healthy. Come on.
Let's go get some lunch.

1158
01:36:33,130 --> 01:36:35,090
After you, Senator.

1159
01:36:36,630 --> 01:36:38,550
Thanks for stopping by, Carl.

1160
01:36:39,430 --> 01:36:41,180
Give us a second.

1161
01:36:41,260 --> 01:36:43,970
What are you doing? Do you want
to be President of the United States

1162
01:36:44,010 --> 01:36:46,730
- or brain dead, stuck full of tubes?
- I'll take my chances.

1163
01:36:46,810 --> 01:36:50,020
I told you your chances don't exist.
We shut down your lab.

1164
01:36:50,100 --> 01:36:51,310
You don't think some smartass on NZT

1165
01:36:51,400 --> 01:36:52,980
might have two or three
or even four labs?

1166
01:36:53,020 --> 01:36:55,230
- So what? You're not making any more.
- That's right, I'm not.

1167
01:36:55,320 --> 01:36:57,360
I thought better of it.
See, once you know what's in it,

1168
01:36:57,440 --> 01:37:01,030
you can tweak it, re-engineer it.
Get the bugs out. Taper off.

1169
01:37:01,780 --> 01:37:03,120
I'm off it, Carl.

1170
01:37:03,200 --> 01:37:04,450
Well, on it or off it,

1171
01:37:04,540 --> 01:37:06,830
there's no scenario
where you're not working for me.

1172
01:37:06,910 --> 01:37:08,410
How you gonna pay me?
In cigarettes?

1173
01:37:08,500 --> 01:37:09,670
Last year you traded

1174
01:37:09,750 --> 01:37:11,670
- inside information 17 times.
- You threatening me?

1175
01:37:11,750 --> 01:37:14,840
Blank checks from Dubai.
Dumping in Chile. Disappeared officials.

1176
01:37:14,920 --> 01:37:17,010
- I mean, you shed a spot light...
- Do you want to go to war with me?

1177
01:37:17,050 --> 01:37:19,170
And trust me when I say this,
I'm not even being creative.

1178
01:37:19,260 --> 01:37:21,220
There's no way you can be making
accusations like that

1179
01:37:21,300 --> 01:37:23,640
and be walking around alive.

1180
01:37:23,720 --> 01:37:26,310
That you would even think that shows me
how unprepared you are to fight me.

1181
01:37:26,390 --> 01:37:27,930
- You're no longer equipped to fight me.
- I may be off it,

1182
01:37:28,020 --> 01:37:30,390
but you think I didn't learn anything?
That my synapses didn't change?

1183
01:37:30,480 --> 01:37:31,810
That I kept absolutely nothing?

1184
01:37:31,900 --> 01:37:33,480
You have no idea what
kind of enemy I can be.

1185
01:37:33,560 --> 01:37:36,400
- That van's gonna rear-end that taxi.
- What?

1186
01:37:36,480 --> 01:37:39,150
The driver's distracted. He's texting.
He's going 30 miles an hour.

1187
01:37:39,240 --> 01:37:41,030
He has 60 feet to stop.
He doesn't have room.

1188
01:37:41,070 --> 01:37:42,870
What van?

1189
01:37:46,200 --> 01:37:47,580
I see everything, Carl.

1190
01:37:47,660 --> 01:37:49,960
I'm 50 moves ahead of you
and everybody else.

1191
01:37:50,040 --> 01:37:52,370
What makes you think I don't have a guy
with a bead on you right now?

1192
01:37:52,460 --> 01:37:54,170
How do you know you're gonna be alive
this time next year?

1193
01:37:54,250 --> 01:37:57,380
What are you doing? You get
your hands off me. What are you doing?

1194
01:37:57,460 --> 01:38:00,220
Something's pumping half-mass in there.
The walls of your heart are dilated.

1195
01:38:00,300 --> 01:38:03,510
Your aortic valve's steno. So you're
gonna need to get that replaced.

1196
01:38:05,300 --> 01:38:06,930
But you already knew that.

1197
01:38:12,730 --> 01:38:14,560
You know, you should
really be glad about this.

1198
01:38:14,650 --> 01:38:17,690
'Cause me working for you?
You'd end up as my bitch.

1199
01:38:29,660 --> 01:38:32,410
Maybe we should cool off.
Talk again.

1200
01:38:34,250 --> 01:38:36,130
I don't think so.

1201
01:38:37,710 --> 01:38:38,960
Don't forget to vote.

1202
01:38:47,260 --> 01:38:48,680
Edward.

1203
01:38:48,760 --> 01:38:50,390
You good?

1204
01:38:50,470 --> 01:38:53,440
Yeah. Told you I have a lunch.

1205
01:38:59,360 --> 01:39:01,740
- Sorry I'm late.
- Hey. Don't worry.

1206
01:39:03,820 --> 01:39:06,370
- How's your day going?
- Ah. I've had better.

1207
01:39:08,160 --> 01:39:10,160
I still can't deal with that haircut.

1208
01:39:10,290 --> 01:39:12,370
I wouldn't even
recognize you on the street.

1209
01:39:12,450 --> 01:39:14,160
Well, I'm a new man.

1210
01:39:15,540 --> 01:39:16,540
I've already ordered.

1211
01:39:32,470 --> 01:39:34,060
What?

